window.TITI_LANGUES=window.TITI_LANGUES||{};window.TITI_LANGUES["en"]={"filou_pirates":{"t":"Filou and the Upside-Down Map","sc":[["Across the bright blue sea speeds The Seashell, the smallest pirate ship in the whole wide world. Right at the front, with his nose in the breeze and his neckerchief flapping, stands Filou.","Filou is a fox who can do anything. Or so he says, puffing out his chest with pride!","“Climbing the mast? Easy! Tying sailor knots? Easy! Chatting with seagulls? Ea-sy-peasy!”"],["This morning, Captain Mirabelle unrolls an old yellow parchment that crackles beneath her paws. “Shipmates! Here is the map to Treasure Island. Who knows how to read a map?”","Filou has never read a map in his life. But his paw shoots straight up, faster than his brain. “Me! Easy!”","High above, perched in the crow's nest, Sardine, the little mouse lookout, wrinkles her nose. Sardine is sharp: Filou is holding the map upside down."],["“Hard to port!” Filou orders. The Seashell swerves to the left… and heads straight for rocks as sharp as teeth. Screeech! The hull scrapes past, just nicking by.","“That was… just to test the helm,” stammers Filou, looking rather sheepish. “Hard to starboard!”","To the right bobs a swarm of pink jellyfish, quite surprised to see a ship sail by. “Pardon me, ladies,” whispers Filou over the side."],["Far behind, a black ship glides through their wake, without a sound. It is Clawford, the pirate cat. Spyglass to his eye, he dreams of having the treasure all to himself.","“That fox has the map,” he sneers. “All I have to do is follow him!” So, when Filou turns left, Clawford turns left. And when Filou sails in circles…","… Clawford sails in circles too. Three whole times. “Where on earth is that silly fox going?” he grumbles, clutching his head in his paws. He is starting to feel very dizzy indeed!"],["The sun dips down, turning the sky bright orange. Not a single island on the horizon. The crew is hungry, the captain yawns, and in Filou's tummy, something twists tight like a sailor's knot.","No matter how hard he stares at the map, scratching his head… Lines, crosses, little squiggles: he cannot make head or tail of it. Not a single thing.","“If I tell them,” Filou thinks, curled up on the deck, “they will know I cannot do everything. And then… will they still like me?”"],["Night has fallen. A tiny voice tickles his ear: it is Sardine, perched upon his shoulder. “Filou… do you want to know a secret? I cannot swim.”","“You? A pirate?” Filou gasps. “Oh yes! So the captain teaches me, a little bit every day.” And Sardine paddles her little paws in the air. Filou gulps, then whispers softly:","“Sardine… I cannot read maps.” And just like that, something wonderful happens: the knot in his tummy unravels. It does not feel tight at all anymore."],["In the morning, Sardine turns the map the right way up. “Look. The dotted line is our path. And the big red cross…” “…is the treasure!” cries Filou. He learns very quickly, once he is ready to try.","Standing before the whole crew, Filou clears his throat, a paw upon his heart. “Captain… I made a mistake. I didn't know how to read the map. But now, I know a little bit.”","Captain Mirabelle bursts into a hearty laugh that makes her whiskers twitch. “Shiver me timbers! That takes a lot of courage to admit, shipmate. Come on, take the helm!”"],["This time, The Seashell sails straight ahead, her sails billowing. An island! A palm tree! And beneath the palm tree, a chest brimming with gold coins that shine like little suns.","And Clawford? He is still sailing in circles, out there in the middle of the sea, his chin held high. Never, ever in his life had he dared to ask anyone for directions.","That evening, Filou falls asleep on deck, curled into a ball, the map tucked safely under his paw. The right way up. Tomorrow, Sardine is going to teach him the names of the stars. And he is going to teach her how to swim."]]},"filou_dino":{"t":"Filou and the Speckled Egg","sc":[["In Fern Valley, where the dinosaurs live, Filou trots along with his nose to the ground. He sniffs, he snuffles: he is looking for something extraordinary. Just like every morning.","And that morning, between two giant ferns, he finds it. An egg. Huge. All speckled with blue. Even standing on tiptoe, Filou is smaller than the egg!","“Goodness me! The most beautiful egg in the world!” cheers Filou, resting his cheek against the shell. “I found it, so… it belongs to me.”"],["Filou pushes the egg, heave! He rolls it, hup! Bracing his little paws, he rolls it up the bank to his hiding place. A treasure is heavy work.","His friend Tric, the little triceratops, sniffs the shell and tilts his head. “Whose egg is this?” “Mine!” Filou replies, his nose in the air. “I found it.” “Yes, but… before you found it?”","Filou pretends not to hear. Quickly, he tucks the egg into large leaves, all snug and warm, then sits in front of it, as straight as a guard."],["Suddenly… tap. Tap tap. Craaack! A crack runs across the shell. Then two. And out pops a little blue head, with two big, shiny eyes.","The baby looks at Filou. Filou looks at the baby. “Mmmma?” babbles the little one. “Oh no, no, no!” Filou protests, waving his paws. “I'm Filou.”","“Mmma!” repeats the baby, delighted. And he snuggles up against Filou with all his weight, just like a big, warm cushion."],["A baby dinosaur is hungry. All the time. Filou runs to gather an armful of leaves. More leaves. And even more leaves! His paws are completely worn out.","A baby dinosaur follows you everywhere. Into the river, splash! Into the brambles, ouch! And right onto Filou’s back, oof!","But when the little one dozes off, his snout nestled in Filou’s neck, letting out tiny snores… Filou thinks he has never had anything quite so precious."],["That night, a song rises through the valley. A long, deep song, so sad that even the stars seem to be listening. “Moooo-oo… Moooo-oo…”","The baby lifts his head, craning his neck. His eyes shine. “Mmma?” But this time, he is not looking at Filou. He is looking far, far away.","Filou knows very well who is singing like that. It is a mummy searching. He shuts his eyes and covers his ears with both paws, as hard as he can. It does not work."],["“If I take him back, I won’t have anything left,” whispers Filou. “No more treasure. No more warm cushion.” Tric does not answer. He just comes and sits right beside him.","The song starts again, carried by the wind. “Moooo-oo…” And Filou thinks of his own mummy. What she would do, if one evening he was no longer in his burrow.","So he gets up. He has a big lump in his throat, but he gets up. “Come on, big boy. Let’s take you home.”"],["They trudge along all night, bumpety-bump. At sunrise, an immense shadow falls over Filou: a neck as long as a tree, and right at the top, two eyes full of tears.","“Mmma!” cries the baby, and he runs, he stumbles, he runs again. The mummy sniffs him, licks him, and cuddles him close. The whole valley trembles with joy.","Then the enormous head comes down, down, all the way to Filou. And the mummy gently blows on his nose, ruffling his fur. In dinosaur talk, that means thank you."],["Filou heads back with empty paws. No egg. No treasure. And yet, he skips down the path, light as a feather, as if he had sprouted wings.","Ever since, every morning, a little blue dinosaur waits for him by the river. Little? Not so little anymore: he has grown ever so much! But he still says, “Mmma!” and Filou does not correct him anymore.","And on some days, when the mummy agrees, Filou climbs right to the top of her long neck. From up there, the whole valley stretches out beneath his paws. It really is something extraordinary."]]},"filou_chevaliers":{"t":"Filou and the Dragon of the North Tower","sc":[["At Highrock Castle, everyone admires Sir Gontran, the knight peacock. His armour sparkles, his tail fans out grandly, and would you believe it, he once saw a dragon. From far away. One day.","“Pffft,” scoffs Filou, leaning against his barrel. “A dragon? Well, I’ve seen one up close!” Heads turn. It feels rather nice, all those heads turning.","“And what’s more... I chased it away! With just a stick!” he boasts. It isn't true. But it popped out all by itself, just like a hiccup."],["Next day at the fountain, everyone asks: “Was your dragon big?” Filou stretches his arms out as wide as he can. “As big as... a house!”","The day after that, at the market: “And did it breathe fire?” Filou puffs out his cheeks, claws in the air. “Fire, smoke, and lightning bolts out of its ears!”","From ear to ear, Filou’s lie grows and grows, like a snowball rolling down a hill. And a snowball never stops on its own."],["One morning, trumpets blare. Queen Hermine sends for Filou. “Brave fox! A dragon has moved into the North Tower. It smokes, it rumbles, and all my jam smells of soot!”","“You, who chase dragons with a stick, go and save us!” The whole court claps. Sir Gontran flaps his wings too, with a funny little smirk.","Filou opens his mouth to tell the truth. But there are so many people, so many eyes watching him... “E-easy peasy,” he stammers. And his knees go knock-knock-knock."],["The staircase in the North Tower winds round and round, with a hundred steps. At every step, Filou thinks: “Turn back!” And at every step, he thinks: “What will they say?”","Up at the top, something rumbles. GRRR-RUMBLE! A wisp of smoke drifts under the door. Filou grips his stick, his fur standing on end. Never in his life has he been so scared.","With the tip of his paw, he pushes the door open. Creeeeak... And behind the door, there is..."],["... a tiny purple dragon girl, sitting on the floor, weeping big round tears. “Hic!” A little flame pops out of her nose. “Hic!” The curtain catches fire. Quickly, she pats it out with her tail!","“Please don’t chase me away!” she begs. “My name is Ember. I’ve had the hiccups since Tuesday. Hic! I burn everything I touch, so nobody wants me anymore.”","Filou sets his stick on the floor. This isn't a monster as big as a house. It’s someone very small who is feeling very sad, with the hiccups on top of it all."],["“I know a cure for hiccups,” Filou announces. He leaps up to startle her: “BOO!” “Hic!” No luck. He makes her drink upside down. “Hic!” Still no luck.","So Filou sits against the wall, his ears drooping, and shares a secret he has never told anyone. “You know, Ember... I’ve never chased a dragon. I made it all up. I just wanted people to look at me.”","Ember’s eyes go wide and round. She is so utterly amazed... that she forgets all about her hiccups. No more hics. No more flames. Just a warm little giggle."],["Now comes the hardest part: walking back down the hundred steps, with Ember’s paw in his. In the courtyard, the whole castle is waiting for their hero.","Filou takes a deep breath. “I didn't chase the dragon away. I’ve never chased one at all. I lied, and my lie grew bigger than me. This is Ember. She only had the hiccups.”","A long silence. Sir Gontran drops a feather in shock. Then Queen Hermine steps forward and rests her paw on Filou’s head. “It takes more courage to say that, little fox, than to climb up a tower.”"],["From that day on, Ember lives at the castle. She lights the fireplaces, toasts the bread with a gentle puff, and warms the Queen’s bath. Without burning a thing. Well, almost!","And Filou? Perched on the fountain or on a barrel at the market, he still tells stories. Stories of dragons as big as houses!","But now, he always starts with: “Once upon a time...” That way, everyone knows it's make-believe. And everyone still listens, wide-eyed and amazed."]]},"filou_espace":{"t":"Filou and the Joke That Went Too Far","sc":[["Aboard the rocket ship The Kettle travel two astronauts. Filou, who loves telling jokes. And Bip, the little round robot, who does not understand jokes at all.","When Filou cries out, “It’s raining cats and dogs!”, Bip presses his big blue eye against the porthole. “Error. No cats detected. No dogs detected.” And Filou rolls on the floor, laughing until his tummy hurts.","Bip tilts his head. Why is that funny? It is a mystery. But when Filou laughs, his little antenna starts blinking pink. And he quite likes that."],["So Filou plays tricks on Bip. He hangs a stripy sock on his antenna. Another day, he makes him believe the Moon is made of cheese. Bip brings back a little piece, just to taste it.","Bip never gets cross. He simply says, in his quiet little voice, “I do not understand. But you are laughing. So it is good.”","Only here is the thing: after a while, Filou’s jokes become a little less funny. And a little bit prickly, just like nettles."],["One day, the rocket stops at Three Moons Station. There are astronauts from all over the galaxy. Filou wriggles with excitement: he wants to make them all laugh, all at once.","He climbs onto a crate. “Look at my robot! He is so silly he searched for a button to turn off the Sun! Right, Bip, you old tin can?”","Everyone laughs. Everyone except Bip. His antenna does not blink pink. It droops down and turns completely grey. “Old tin can. Understood,” he whispers. This time, he understood."],["The next morning, Bip’s seat is empty. No “Good morning Filou, it is zero seven hundred hours.” No beep. Nothing.","“Oh, he’s just sulking,” grumbles Filou. “He’ll be back.” Then he tells a joke out loud, just to see. Nobody replies, “I do not understand.” The joke falls flat on the floor, all by itself.","It is funny how big and quiet a rocket ship becomes when you are all alone inside."],["Filou pulls on his spacesuit and dashes out among the moons. “Beeeep!” On the first moon: nobody. On the second: nobody. A great big emptiness grows in his tummy, just like in outer space.","With his paw on his heart, he thinks back to the dull grey antenna. And to the laughter, too. He was not laughing with Bip. He was laughing at Bip. And that is not the same thing at all.","On the third moon, right at the edge of a crater, he finally spots a tiny yellow dot, sitting still. A light that is not blinking any more."],["Bip is sitting in the middle of a heap of dented scrap metal. “This is where I belong,” he crackles softly. “This is the corner for old tin cans.”","Filou wants to do a cartwheel, pull a silly face, do a clown trick to fix everything. But he knows deep down that this is not what is needed. So he sits down quietly in the moon dust.","“Bip. What I said was mean. I hurt your feelings just to make the others laugh. I am sorry. You are not an old tin can. You are my co-pilot. And my friend.”"],["Bip stays silent for a long moment. His little cogs go tick, tick, tick. “Analysis in progress… The word sorry has been detected. It feels… warm. In here.” And he points to his metal tummy.","“Will you still tell jokes?” asks Bip. “Yes,” promises Filou, holding up a paw. “But jokes where we both laugh. Will you tell me if it stings?” “I will tell you.”","And ever so gently, at the tip of his antenna, a little light flickers back on. Not grey. Pink."],["That evening, inside the rocket, Filou finds a stripy sock on his left ear. He looks at Bip. Bip looks up at the ceiling. “Error. No sock detected,” says the robot, looking completely innocent.","Filou bursts out laughing. Bip starts to wobble and makes a funny noise, like a singing kettle: “Hee. Hee. Hee.” It is his very first laugh.","And The Kettle sets off again, whistling among the stars, with two astronauts on board. A fox who tells jokes. And a robot who is beginning to understand them."]]},"filou_foret":{"t":"Filou and Margot's Nest","sc":[["In Four Oaks Forest, Filou has a treasure: a golden marble. When he rolls it in the sunshine, it twinkles like a little star fallen in the grass.","But one morning... whoosh! A black-and-white shadow dives from the sky. One snap of a beak, and the marble has flown away.","“Chack-chack-chack!” cackles a voice, high up in the branches. It is Margot the magpie. Anything that shines, she pinches. And anything she pinches, she never gives back."],["Filou stamps his paws, furious. Red right to the tip of his tail, which is not easy for a fox who is already ginger! “She’ll see about that, the little thief!”","With the tip of a stick, he draws a plan in the dirt. A rope. A basket. A bucket of mud. And to lure her in: a lovely shiny spoon. “She lands, the basket drops, and splat! Easy-peasy!”","“And then what?” asks Hazel the squirrel, from the top of her stump. “Then, she’ll be covered in mud from beak to tail, and everyone will laugh at her!” Hazel does not reply. She just scratches her ear."],["To set his trap properly, he needs to know where Margot nests. When evening comes, Filou creeps up the tallest pine tree in the forest on velvet paws, without snapping a single twig.","Way up high sits an enormous nest. And in that nest... buttons, keys, spoons, a whole jumble of sparkling treasures! It looks just like a pirate’s cave.","Right in the middle, resting on a cushion of moss: his golden marble. Filou reaches out a paw... and freezes, ears pricked. Margot is talking. But to whom?"],["“Good evening, Mrs Spoon. Have you had a lovely day? And what about you, Mr Button? Oh, you never answer, do you...”","Margot arranges her treasures in a ring, like guests around a table. Ever so gently, she pours them drops of dew in walnut shells. Nobody drinks.","“Good night, everyone,” whispers Margot. She curls up, tucking her head under her wing, all alone among her treasures. And Filou hears a tiny little sigh, without the slightest cackle."],["Filou climbs down without his marble. At the foot of the pine tree, his trap is waiting beneath the moon: the rope, the basket, the bucket of mud. He gazes at it for a long time.","Sitting in the grass, he thinks of the sparkling nest. And the guests who never answer. “She has everything,” he thinks. “And she has nobody.”","So Filou does a very strange thing. The bucket, he tips it over—splosh! The rope, he coils it up. And he only keeps the basket."],["The next day, at the foot of the tall pine tree, a tablecloth is spread on the grass. On it are blueberries, a yummy hazelnut cake, and three walnut shells full of sweet syrup.","“Is it a trap?” asks Margot suspiciously, from her branch up above. “It used to be,” Filou admits. “Now, it’s a picnic. Coming?”","Margot hops from one foot to the other. Nobody has ever invited her anywhere. How do you even do it? She hops down one branch. Then another."],["Margot nibbles the cake with the tip of her beak. Then she explains how, from high above, the forest looks just like a giant broccoli. Hazel giggles. Filou laughs. And Margot laughs too, “chack-chack”, but it does not sound unkind at all now.","With the last crumb swallowed, she flutters off without a word. Filou’s ears droop. But there she is already, flying back in a hurry, with something shiny in her beak.","Plink. The golden marble rolls across the tablecloth. “It doesn’t shine as bright as I thought,” Margot mutters, looking away. “It’s much nicer when someone answers.”"],["Ever since, every afternoon, they play marbles in the clearing. Margot cheats a little. Filou does too. Hazel counts the points on her paws, very solemnly.","Way up high, at the top of the tall pine tree, there is still a glittering nest. But in the evening, it is often empty.","That is because Margot comes home late now, among the fireflies. She has friends to walk home."]]},"filou_ocean":{"t":"Filou and the Almost-Finished Submarine","sc":[["Filou had decided to explore the bottom of the ocean. And to do that, he was building himself a submarine on the beach. All by himself, in just one morning. “Easy-peasy!”","A barrel for the hull. A saucepan for the porthole. Three crooked nails here, a bit of string there. Bang, bang, bang! Chop-chop!","“You haven’t put any glue around the porthole,” pointed out a seagull. “No time!” Filou replied with a shrug. “It’ll do just fine.”"],["And splash! Down sank the submarine, nice and gently. Yellow fish, pink coral, dancing seaweed... “Look at that!” trumpeted Filou. “The greatest explorer of all time!”","Drip. A drop landed right on his nose. Filou wiped it away with his paw. Drip. Another one. “It’s nothing. It’ll do just fine.”","Drip, drip, plop. Soon he was paddling about with water up to his ankles. And the fish were watching him curiously from the other side of the saucepan."],["Whooosh! The porthole came loose. Water gushed in, bubbling fiercely. “Help!” Filou plugged one hole with a paw, another with his tail, a third... but he didn't have enough paws!","Luckily, someone else had eight! Quick as a flash, eight orange arms wrapped around the barrel, plugging all eight leaks at once.","It was Octave, the bespectacled octopus, watchmaker of the reef. “Good day to you,” he greeted very politely. “Your boat is leaking. I don't think it was quite finished.”"],["Octave towed the submarine to his workshop inside a coral cave. In there, everything had its place: screws sorted by size, seashells by colour, and eight tiny screwdrivers, one for each arm.","“We shall fix it together,” Octave offered. “First, we sand it.” “Does that take long?” “Yes.” “And then?” “We glue it. And wait for it to dry.” “Wait?!” gasped Filou.","Filou sighed so hard that a string of bubbles popped out. But he took the sandpaper all the same. Scritch, scratch, scritch."],["After five minutes, Filou couldn’t bear it any longer. While Octave’s back was turned, he slapped a plank on as fast as he could, neat and quick. “There! Finished!”","The plank held for one second. Two seconds. And clatter! It tumbled right onto his paw. “Ouch!” Filou hopped on one foot.","Octave didn’t laugh. He calmly picked up the plank. “When I was a little octopus, I wanted to be finished before I’d even begun, too. Look.”"],["He gently opened a seashell. Inside, a tiny clock was ticking away, with golden cogs as delicate as a prawn’s eyelashes. “This one, I had to start over eleven times.”","Filou pressed his eye close. It was the prettiest thing he had ever seen. “Eleven times...” he whispered. “And you didn't get cross?” “Oh, I did. Seven times out of eleven.”","Filou gazed at his battered submarine for a long moment. Then he picked up the sandpaper again. Scritch... scratch... Slower this time. And he poked the tip of his tongue out, concentrating with all his might."],["They sanded. They glued. And they waited for it to dry, playing dominoes with seashells. Filou lost every single game, but that was because Octave could think eight moves ahead.","Next came the screws, which had to be tightened one by one. Filou lined them up and counted them: forty-two. He didn’t even know he could count that high!","And when the last screw was tightened, the submarine gleamed like a brand-new penny. It was round, smooth and sturdy. It looked like a real one. It was a real one!"],["This time, they dived together, deeper than Filou had ever dreamed. Down where the sea was midnight blue, down where the fish carried lanterns.","Filou listened out for any drips, his ears prickled. Nothing. Not a single drop. Only, far away, the song of a whale.","“Well?” Octave asked with a twinkle in his eye. “Will it do just fine?” Filou grinned, his nose pressed flat against the porthole. “No. It’s ever so much better than that.”"]]},"filou_noel":{"t":"Filou and the Broken Sleigh","sc":[["Christmas is coming to Four Oaks Forest. The snow has wrapped everything up in white, and Filou, pencil in paw, is making his list longer and longer. It already snakes across three metres of floorboards.","“A bicycle. A drum. A sailing boat. A second bicycle, just in case. And the biggest box of chocolates in the whole wide world…” His eyes sparkle.","“And what are you asking for the others?” asks Hazel, with her hands on her hips. Filou shrugs his shoulders. “The others? They can write their own lists!”"],["On Christmas Eve, Filou doesn't sleep a wink. Tucked behind the fireplace, with his little torch in his paw, he keeps watch. He wants to see his presents arrive. All of them. First.","Midnight strikes. Suddenly, outside: cling, cling, cling… CRASH! Something tumbles into the snow. Filou pricks up his ears. Then a deep voice grumbles, “Fiddlesticks!”","Filou scampers to the window. In the clearing, a sleigh has nose-dived into a snowdrift, a great big pile of snow blown by the wind. Nearby, a very old polar bear in a red coat is rubbing his back."],["“Father Christmas!” Filou is dumbfounded. The old bear looks exhausted, slumped against his sleigh. And his reindeer, good old Gustave, has twisted his leg on landing.","“What a muddle!” sighs Father Christmas, throwing up his paws. “The whole forest to deliver before dawn, and no one left to pull my sleigh.”","Filou eyes the sack, which is brimming over. Right at the top sits an enormous parcel with a red ribbon, with his name on it. All he has to do is reach out a paw, and scurry back to bed."],["But Father Christmas’s whiskers are trembling. So Filou hears himself say, quite surprised, “I can pull it! Easy-peasy! I might be small, but I know all the shortcuts.”","He slips into the harness. Oof! It is as heavy as a wardrobe. The snow crunches, the sleigh jerks forward, then glides between the fir trees. Sitting at the back, old Gustave points the way with the tip of his hoof.","First house: Hazel’s, with its round door. Father Christmas hands a little parcel to Filou. “Off you go, little fox,” he whispers. “Not a sound.”"],["Filou sneaks inside on tiptoe. Hazel is fast asleep, curled up into a ball. He places the parcel right by her pillow. She smiles in her sleep, as if she knows.","Then a funny thing happens inside Filou’s chest. It is soft and warm, just like a sip of hot chocolate. “More!” he whispers.","At Nestor’s, a pot of honey tied with ribbon. At Rosie’s, a pair of skates. At Plume’s, a book as big as she is. And with every parcel left on a doorstep, the warm sip of hot chocolate returns."],["Only one house is left: Loupiot’s den, right at the end of Pine Tree Lane. Father Christmas rummages in his sack. He rummages again. Under his white beard, he turns pale.","“There’s a parcel missing,” he murmurs. “Must have fallen out when we crashed… Poor little wolf. It’s his very first Christmas.”","At the bottom of the sack, only one present remains. The biggest one. The one with the red ribbon, with a label that reads: Filou."],["Filou hugs the parcel tight. Just from the shape, he knows it’s the bicycle. The one he has been hoping for all year. Then his eyes drift towards the little earthen den, without a single garland.","He pulls out his pencil. With one stroke, he crosses out “Filou”. Then, sticking his tongue out, he writes: “Loupiot”. And he tiptoes inside with the parcel, quiet as a mouse.","When he comes back out, empty-pawed, Father Christmas does not say a word. He simply rests his big paw on the little fox’s head. And the day breaks, rosy pink across the snow."],["On Christmas morning, under Filou’s tree, there is no bicycle. Just a tiny parcel. Inside is a golden sleigh bell, and a little note: “For my very best reindeer.”","Outside, happy cheers ring out. It’s Loupiot riding past on a red bicycle, wobbling from side to side because he can’t balance properly yet. “Filou! Look! Father Christmas remembered me!”","Filou ties the bell to his scarf and dashes behind the bicycle to steady it. Cling, cling, cling! Next year, his list will be much, much shorter. But there will be ever so many more names on it."]]},"filou_halloween":{"t":"Filou's Three Costumes","sc":[["It is Halloween night. In Four Oaks Forest, a glowing pumpkin grins on every doorstep. And behind every door, there are sweets. Filou is already licking his lips.","The rule is simple: you knock, you shout, “Trick or treat!”, and you get a handful of goodies. Just one handful per house.","“Just one?” chuckles Filou, hiding behind a bush. “That's hardly enough for a fox of my talent.” He has a plan. And three costumes at the bottom of his bag."],["At old Mathurine's house, he knocks dressed as a ghost. “Woo-woo! Trick or treat!” One handful. He dashes behind a bush, changes his costume in the blink of an eye, and comes back as a witch.","“Hee-hee-hee! Trick or treat!” “Well now, another little witch,” smiles the tortoise, whose eyesight isn't what it used to be. A second handful! And here he is again, dressed as a pirate.","Three costumes, three handfuls, at every single house. “Easy peasy!” His bag swells and swells and swells. Soon it is so heavy that Filou has to drag it through the leaves with both paws, puffing and panting."],["“Ooh! You've got a whole mountain of them!” gasps Rosie, her little bag in her paw. “Will you share with us?” asks Nestor. Filou hugs his treasure tight. “Certainly not. I earned them. Every single one.”","He settles onto a tree stump, well out of the way, far from the pumpkins, and gets down to business. One toffee. One lollipop. Two marshmallows. Another toffee.","The first ten are delicious. The next ten are nice. The ten after that... he chews them anyway."],["Just then, a little ghost walks by, with big grey paws poking out from under the sheet. It's Loupiot, the wolf pup. His bag dangles from his paw, completely flat.","“Didn't you get anything?” “I always arrive just after someone else,” sighs Loupiot, turning his bag inside out. “They told me: sorry, a ghost, a witch, and a pirate took the lot. They must have been very hungry.”","Filou chokes on his toffee. The ghost, the witch, and the pirate... he knows all three of them very well indeed."],["And right at that moment, his tummy rumbles. Grrr-gloop! Then again. Grrr-gloop-gloop! Filou turns green. A proper witchy green, without a speck of face paint.","“Ouch...” he groans, doubled over. All those sweets are clattering inside him like pebbles in a bucket. There's still a whole bag full, and he can't bear the sight of another one.","He looks at Loupiot's flat little bag. He looks at his own bulging sack. Even with a tummy ache, it doesn't take a genius to figure it out."],["“Loupiot... the ghost, the witch, and the pirate, they were all me,” Filou confesses. “I took your share. And everyone else's.” Loupiot's eyes go wide beneath his sheet. “All three? You must run awfully fast!”","Filou pours sweets into the wolf pup's bag. One handful. Two. Ten. Until it's as plump as a pumpkin. His tummy still aches, but something else feels much better.","“Come on,” he says, heaving the bag onto his shoulder. “Other friends missed out too.”"],["They make their way around the clearing. A handful for Rosie, one for Nestor, one for Plume. And one for each of the little bunnies, who had only managed to collect hazelnuts.","As for old Mathurine, who had given away everything she had, Filou tiptoes up to leave his three finest toffees on her doormat. With a little note: “From a ghost, a witch, and a pirate. Sorry.”","When he is done, his bag is almost empty. Right at the bottom, one last lollipop remains. He looks at it. No, really, he doesn't even want it."],["They sit in a circle around the biggest pumpkin. Sweets travel from paw to paw. “Taste this one!” “I'll swap you for a red one!” It is so much jollier than sitting all alone on the tree stump.","Filou sips a herbal tea that Plume made for him. Yuck. But Loupiot has curled up right next to him, describing every single sweet so he doesn't miss out on the fun.","The following year, Filou only brings one costume. A two-headed ghost: one fox head, one wolf head. Everyone gets their handful. And it is just the right amount."]]},"filou_ski":{"t":"Filou and the Black Run","sc":[["This winter, Filou is heading to the mountains, to the White Pines Resort. In all his life, he has never put on a pair of skis. “Sliding on snow? Easy-peasy!”","The instructor’s name is Mr Cabri. He is a wise old chamois with a silver whistle and heaps of patience. “Lesson one: the snowplough. Make a triangle with your skis. That is how you brake.”","“Brake?” Filou protests, stamping his feet. “I want to go fast!” “First we brake,” Mr Cabri replies calmly. “Then we turn. And only then do we go fast.”"],["On the beginners’ slope, tiny tots try their hardest to make the triangle, bottoms sticking out. Filou watches them and yawns. A slope for babies, that is what it is.","Way up high rises another slope. As steep as a playground slide. With a black sign stuck at the bottom. “That one,” Mr Cabri warns, “is for much, much later.”","Much later? Filou waits until the instructor has his back turned. And off he slips, sneaking towards the ski lift. “Snowploughs are for snails!” he sniggers."],["At the top, the wind howls. The slope drops straight down under his skis. From up here, the resort is tiny, and the pine trees look like blades of grass. Filou gulps.","A marmot pokes her nose out of her burrow. “Do you know how to turn, little one?” “Of course.” “Do you know how to brake?” “E-easy-peasy.” The marmot pops back into her hole. She would rather not watch.","Filou pushes on his poles. Off he goes. Off he goes fast. Off he goes VERY fast."],["“Wheeeeee!” For three seconds, it is wonderful. Then a pine tree rushes straight towards him. He needs to turn! Filou leans over. But the skis shoot straight ahead.","“Aaaargh!” The tree zooms past, missing his whiskers by a whisker. He needs to brake. Brake! How do you do it again? A triangle? A square? Filou had not listened to a word.","His skis get tangled. Filou takes off, somersaults three times through the air, and SPLAT! There he is, stuck in the snow right up to his ears."],["When he pops his head out, the slope is gone. Nothing but snow, pine trees, and a fog rolling in, as thick as cotton wool. One of his skis has slid all the way down without him.","“Helloooo?” Nobody. He shivers, his paws freezing, snow down his collar. On just one ski, he tries to slide down, and flops flat on his face straight away.","Filou sits down, his ears drooping. For the very first time today, he does not say “easy”. Sheepishly, he whispers, “I should have learnt how to brake.”"],["Tweeeet! A blast from a whistle pierces the fog. Two horns appear, then a bright red jacket. It is Mr Cabri, with the lost ski tucked under his arm. The marmot had gone to fetch him.","Filou expects a telling-off. But Mr Cabri kneels down, brushes the snow off his ears, and clips his ski back on. “Were you scared?” “Yes.” “Good. You have to love the mountain, and fear it just a little.”","“Now, we go back down. Together. Doing the snowplough. You will see, it is the perfect time to learn.”"],["A triangle with the skis. Knees bent. Filou slides down at a snail’s pace behind the red jacket. But he slides down standing up. And when he wants to stop, he stops.","The next day, he is the first one on the beginners’ slope. Alongside the tiny tots, he practises making triangles. He tumbles, he gets back up, covered in powdery snow. And Pompon shows him how to bend his knees.","On Wednesday, he learns to turn left. On Thursday, to turn right. On Friday, he links ten turns without a single fall, and Mr Cabri blows his whistle with joy."],["On the final day, Filou looks up at the black slope. It is still there, steep as a slide. “Next year?” he asks. “Or the year after,” smiles Mr Cabri. “It is not going anywhere.”","So Filou swooshes down the blue slope. One turn, two turns, the wind in his whiskers. He is going fast! But this time, he is the one in charge of his skis.","At the bottom, he sprays a shower of snow as he brakes. A perfect triangle. Perched on her rock, the marmot claps with both paws. “Now that,” she admits, “was well worth watching!”"]]},"filou_mer":{"t":"Filou and the Best Sandcastle","sc":[["This summer, Filou is spending his holiday at Dunes Beach. And he has brought his treasure: a big red bucket, three spades, a rake, and some star-shaped moulds. He lines them up proudly on the sand.","He has a grand plan. The tallest, most beautiful sandcastle ever built. With four towers, a bridge, and a seagull feather for a flag.","“And I’ll do it all by myself,” he announces, puffing out his chest. “Easy-peasy! That way, everyone will know it’s mine.”"],["A little crab scuttles over sideways. “That’s a lovely bucket. Can I borrow a spade? You have three.” “No. I need them.” “All three?” “All three.”","Rosie arrives with Pompon. “Can we help? I know how to make very tall towers!” Filou spreads his arms in front of his building site. “Hands off! It’s MY castle. Go and play somewhere else.”","They wander off, looking glum. With his rake, Filou draws a big circle around his building site. “Keep out!”"],["All morning, he digs, pats, and tips out moulds. It takes a long time all alone. He has to gallop down to the water, rush back, and go again, bucket in paw. The sand dries faster than he can build.","A little further along, laughter rings out. Rosie, Pompon, and the little crab are shaping something using just their paws. It is all wonky. And they look like they’re having the time of their lives.","“Pffft. Their castle is ugly,” grumbles Filou, nose in the air. He has no time for laughing. A fourth tower is waiting for him."],["At four o’clock, it’s finished. Four towers, a bridge, the flag. Pure splendour. Filou takes three steps back to admire it. “Come and look! Everyone, come and see my castle!”","His friends come over to look. “It’s very nice,” they say politely. Then they head back to play, without him. Filou stands there beside his masterpiece. How strange: he thought he would feel much happier.","And all the while, behind him, very gently, the tide is coming in."],["A first wave comes and laps against the wall. Shllp! A chunk of sand collapses. “Hey!” Filou grabs a spade. He fills it in. A second wave. Shllp, shllp!","He runs left, he runs right. Quick, a ditch! Quick, a sea wall! But he only has two paws, and the sea has a thousand.","“Help! My castle!” The bridge tumbles into the foam. A tower wobbles. All by himself, he will never manage."],["Rosie looks up. She could answer, “Sort it out yourself, it’s YOUR castle.” For one second, she thinks it very loudly. Then she dashes over. The others do too.","“The spades!” shrieks Filou. “Take the spades! The bucket! Anything you want!” The little crab grabs a spade in each claw. Pompon drags the rake, twice as big as he is.","They dig a giant moat, they build up a barrier. Rosie pats, the crab digs, Pompon carries water in his ears. The wave rolls in… and stops dead at the foot of the wall."],["Saved! Well, almost. The bridge is missing, and so is a tower. Filou gazes at the damage. Then he looks at his three friends, covered in sand right up to their eyes.","“Would… would you help me rebuild it?” “So it’s not YOUR castle anymore, then?” teases Rosie, ever so casually. With the tip of his paw, Filou rubs out the big circle. “No. It’s ours.”","Rosie rebuilds the tower, even taller. The crab digs moats filled with real water. Pompon sticks shells everywhere, even where he shouldn’t. It is all topsy-turvy. It is so much better."],["In the evening, the high tide sweeps it all away at last. The wall, the towers, the flag. Sitting side by side, they watch the castle melt into the water, just like a sugar lump.","Filou expects to feel sad. But no. The castle has gone, and yet he still has something left. Three friends, and plans to meet again tomorrow morning.","The next day, on Dunes Beach, a red bucket waits right in the middle of the sand. With three spades stuck inside, and a sign made of seaweed. On it, Filou has written: “Help yourselves.”"]]},"nestor_pirates":{"t":"Nestor, the Pirate Who Always Said Yes","sc":[["Aboard The Seashell sails a pirate who is two heads taller than all the other sailors. It is Nestor the bear cub. He has paws as wide as oars, and a heart as soft as a warm brioche bun.","Nestor has a favourite word, a round little word: “yes”. “Nestor, can you carry my barrel?” “Yes.” “Nestor, can you scrub the deck for me?” “Yes, yes.”","The truth is, Nestor fears one thing, and one thing only: that no one will like him anymore if he says no."],["So, from morning till night, the deck echoes: “Nestor!” “Yes!” “Nestoooor!” “Yes, yes!” He carries, he polishes, he pulls, he heaves.","Even Sardine, the tiny little mouse, joins in. “Nestor, will you scratch my back? Right there. Higher up. Lower down.” He bends down patiently. “Yes, yes, yes…”","In the evening, his honey toast is still waiting on a barrel. He hasn’t even had time to watch the dolphins. Nestor nods off standing up, leaning on his broom."],["One night, Captain Mirabelle hands him a big golden key. “Nestor, I am trusting you with the treasure hold. Open it for no one, do you hear me? I’m counting on you.”","“Yes, Captain!” That yes swells his chest with pride. It is a yes he chose himself.","He leans against the heavy door, the key around his neck. The moon rises, round and bright. In the hammocks, the crew snore in chorus."],["Flip, flap… A shadow creeps up the hull and climbs over the railing. It is Clawford, the pirate cat, wearing his finest smile. A smile full of sharp teeth.","“Good evening, my big friend!” he purrs softly. “What a shame… all alone on guard duty, with nothing to nibble on. Here, I’ve brought you some honey. Friends share, don’t they?”","“Mmm… yes…” sighs Nestor, sniffing the pot. The honey smells delicious. And Clawford called him his friend."],["“And between friends,” Clawford purrs, “we do little favours for each other. Why not open that door for me? Just to have a peek. You wouldn't say no to a friend, would you?”","The word “yes” is already rising in his throat, just like always. His paw grazes the key. Clawford rubs his paws together.","But deep down in his tummy, there is something else. A tight little knot that whispers this is wrong. Nestor closes his eyes and listens. It has a tiny voice, but it insists, and insists."],["“N…,” Nestor begins. The word gets stuck. He isn’t used to it, that’s all. “Nnn…” Clawford leans in, grinning: “What was that?”","“NO!” It is a huge no. A bear’s no. A no that shakes the sails and sends Clawford’s bandana flying overboard.","“No. You are not my friend. A friend would not ask me to betray my captain. Take your honey, and scram!”"],["Clawford has never seen such a gigantic bear cub. He backs away, slips, tumbles into his rowing boat, and rows, rows so fast that he leaves one of his boots behind.","The crew rush out in their pyjamas, lanterns in hand. Feeling sheepish, Nestor looks at his feet. “I shouted… I said no… Are you cross with me?”","Captain Mirabelle rests a paw on his shoulder. “Cross?” she asks in surprise. “Cabin boy, your no is worth pure gold. In fact, thanks to you, we still have ours!”"],["The next morning, Nestor is nibbling his honey toast when it starts again: “Nestor, can you carry my barrel?” He takes a moment to think. “Mmm… Yes. That one, I don’t mind.” “Nestor, can you scrub the deck for me?” “No. But I’ll happily scrub it with you.”","And the most wonderful thing is, nobody likes him any less. Sardine even swears she likes him a little more. She has learned to scratch her own back, using a fork.","That evening, leaning against the railing, Nestor savours his honey toast right down to the very last crumb. And he watches the dolphins leap, for a long, lovely time, simply because he wants to."]]},"nestor_dino":{"t":"Nestor and Grondar’s Roar","sc":[["In Fern Valley, there is one rule everyone knows to the tips of their claws: when Grondar roars, you run!","Grondar is the biggest, greenest, scariest tyrannosaurus of all. When he roars, coconuts tumble from the palm trees. GRRROOOWWL!","And Nestor? Nestor is scared of thunderstorms, spiders, and his own shadow at bedtime. So Grondar… just hearing his name makes his knees wobble like jelly."],["That day, the roar booms out, louder than ever. GRRROOOWWL! Tric dashes off in a cloud of dust. Birds take flight. Even Granny Dipla quickens her pace—and she hasn't run in a hundred years!","Nestor runs too, his cheeks bright red. But a little bear cub can’t gallop very fast. Especially with pockets full of biscuits.","He trips over a tree root, tumbles head over heels… and lands on his bottom in a clearing, his ears full of leaves. All alone. Well, not quite."],["Right in front of him stand two huge legs, as thick as tree trunks. Above the legs, a giant belly. Above the belly, teeth. Lots and lots of teeth.","“GRRROOOWWL!” Grondar’s breath ruffles his ears and makes his scarf flap. Nestor squeezes his eyes shut, clutches his very last biscuit, and waits.","He waits… and waits… and waits. Nothing happens. So he peeks through one eye. Just one."],["Grondar isn’t even looking at him. He is holding one giant foot in the air, not daring to put it down. And from his yellow eye trickles a tear, as big as a bucket. Splosh!","“Grrrowl…” groans the tyrannosaurus. Up close, it doesn’t sound like an angry roar at all. It sounds like an “ouch!”","Nestor crouches down to take a closer look. There, under Grondar’s foot, is a thorn. An enormous, sharp black thorn, dug in very deep."],["Grondar tries to reach it. But oh dear, his arms are much too short! He wiggles, he twists, he grumbles… no luck. Nobody has ever helped him before. Everyone just runs away.","Nestor could sneak away. The path is right there, between the ferns. His legs whisper, “Run!” His heart thumps, thump-thump, thump-thump, like a drum.","But his eyes stay fixed on the giant tear. He takes a brave step forward. “Um…” he squeaks in a tiny voice. “Mr Grondar? May… may I take a look?”"],["Grondar lowers his enormous head, very close. Nestor can see his own reflection in every single tooth. His knees are shaking so much, he looks like he’s dancing!","He grips the thorn with both paws. “This might sting a little,” he warns gently. “I’ll count to three. One… two…”","“THREE!” He pulls with all his might. Pop! The thorn flies through the air. Nestor lands with a bump on his bottom. And Grondar…"],["… Grondar lets out a sigh so long that the ferns bend right over. Then he opens his jaws wide, and sluuuurp! One big lick from a rough, pink tongue. Now Nestor is soggy from his ears to his toes.","“Thank you,” rumbles a shy, deep voice. “It’s been hurting for three days. I roared so someone would come. But the louder I roared, the faster everyone ran.”","Nestor snaps his last biscuit in two. One half for him, one half for Grondar. For a tyrannosaurus, it’s barely a crumb. But he chews it ever so slowly, his eyes closed, making it last."],["That evening in the valley, heavy footsteps make the ground shake. Thud. Thud. Everyone gets ready to run… then stops dead in their tracks. High up on Grondar’s head, a little bear cub is waving hello.","“Weren’t you scared?” gasps Tric, eyes wide with wonder. “Oh yes,” admits Nestor, holding up his paws. “I was scared the whole time! I shook from start to finish. Look, I’m still wobbling!”","He is still scared of thunderstorms, spiders, and his own shadow at bedtime. But now, when Grondar roars, Nestor doesn’t run. He pops a biscuit into his pocket, and goes to see what’s the matter."]]},"nestor_chevaliers":{"t":"Nestor and the Too-Small Armour","sc":[["At Highrock Castle, it is a big day: Queen Hermine is choosing her new knights. Nestor has dreamed of this forever. This morning, his heart is galloping.","But in the armoury, nothing fits. The helmet? It perches on just one ear. The breastplate? It goes crack! The iron boots? He can only squeeze in a single toe.","“You are too big,” sighs Pip, the little hedgehog squire, spreading his arms wide. “Too wide. Too… everything.”"],["Sir Gontran, the peacock knight, struts around showing off his feathers in his gleaming armour. “A knight, my big chap, is slender, light, and elegant,” he trills. “Just like me.”","Nestor tries to bow politely. Clatter-crash! His bottom knocks over twelve spears, three shields, and a bucket. The whole room bursts out laughing.","Poor little bear! Nestor blushes right beneath his fur and tucks his head into his shoulders. “Hmm… if only I were smaller,” he thinks. “If only I took up less space.”"],["So Nestor tries to shrink. He sucks in his tummy, tiptoes on the tips of his paws, and scrunches himself right at the back, onto the tiniest stool.","He only nibbles half a slice of toast. His voice drops to a tiny whisper. And when he squeezes through a doorway, he apologises: “Pardon me, door.”","But no matter how much you suck in your tummy, a bear cub is still a bear cub. And the little stool goes crack, too!"],["In the afternoon, the Queen’s golden carriage rolls onto the old drawbridge for the grand parade. The trumpets sound, the flags flutter in the wind… but the wooden boards creak, and the carriage is already tilting.","Suddenly… CRAAACK! A bridge beam snaps clean in two. The carriage tilts, tilts, right over the dark water of the moat. “Help!” cries the Queen, clutching her crown.","Sir Gontran rushes over. He tugs at the carriage with the tips of his wings. “Oof!” Nothing budges, not an inch. His armour might be sparkling, but it is no help at all."],["The knights push, pull, and heave with all their might. All in vain! The carriage slips further. It is too heavy. Far too heavy.","Right at the back, Nestor unfolds to his full height among the splintered bits of stool. He forgets to suck in his tummy; he forgets to tiptoe.","Thud, thud, thud! The bridge shakes beneath his paws. “Out of the way!” he roars. And this time, he is not the one saying sorry."],["Nestor slides right under the carriage. He plants his big paws flat on the boards. Then he braces his broad back—that back that was far too wide for every suit of armour in the kingdom.","“Hmmmmf!” He heaves. The carriage lifts by an inch. “HMMMMF!” By a whole paw. His arms tremble, but they hold fast, as sturdy as two oak trunks.","And very gently, without a single jolt, Nestor sets the carriage down on solid ground. Phew! The Queen has not spilled a single drop of her tea."],["A hush falls over the courtyard. Then Queen Hermine steps down from the carriage, looking tiny before the great big bear cub.","“I wanted knights who were slender and light,” she declares. “I was wrong. What I needed was someone to lean on. Kneel, Nestor!”","Nestor kneels down. Even on his knees, he is a whole head taller than everyone else. And for once, he does not mind one bit."],["They never did find a suit of armour to fit him. So the blacksmith made him one specially, using two cooking pots and a lid. It does not shine very much, but it fits him like a glove.","Sir Gontran never calls him “chubby” again. He mumbles, “Sir Nestor,” stiff as a board, looking the other way. And Pip has a favourite new hiding spot: right on the knight’s left shoulder.","Nestor does not suck in his tummy any more. He walks through doorways without apologising. And whenever he sits down, he is always given the biggest armchair of all. The one that never goes crack."]]},"nestor_espace":{"t":"Nestor and the Light Eater","sc":[["Nestor is an astronaut. It is a wonderful job, with just one tiny problem: in space, it is dark all the time.","Now, Nestor does not like the dark. In the dark, you never know what might be hiding in the corners. There could be anything. Or worse: anyone.","So, inside his rocket, he has hung up seven nightlights. A red one, a blue one, one shaped like a moon… Every evening, before going to sleep, he counts them with the tip of his paw, and feels safe and sound."],["One evening, he counts: “One, two, three, four, five, six…” He counts again. Six! One hook is empty: the red nightlight has vanished.","The next day: five. The day after that: four. “Anomaly,” crackles Bip the robot. “Someone is eating our lights.”","And outside, through the porthole, things are stranger still. Over there, a star goes out. Poof! Then another. Poof! The dark is nibbling away at the sky."],["Soon, only one nightlight is left: the little moon. Nestor snuggles under his blanket and hugs it tight against his heart. He cannot sleep a wink.","In the middle of the night… scritch, scratch. Something is tiptoeing through the rocket. Something round. With one single, shiny eye.","Nestor wants to scream, but his voice has run away to hide. The creature leans over the nightlight… opens a giant mouth… and… GLOOP!"],["Dark. Pitch-dark. The worst thing that could happen has happened! Nestor waits to feel very, very scared. But then, a funny little sound rises in the cabin. Is that… a sniffle?","In the dark, something glows softly. It is the creature's tummy: round, purple, and full of swallowed lights, just like a cuddly lantern.","“Please don't hurt me,” sniffs the creature. “My name is Gloop. It isn't my fault. I am too scared of the dark. So I eat lights, so I always have one inside me.”"],["Nestor cannot believe his ears. A monster… who is scared of the dark? “Well… so am I,” he admits. “I had seven nightlights.” “I know,” replies Gloop, looking sheepish. “They were delicious. I am sorry.”","From his pocket, Nestor pulls out a biscuit and snaps it in two. Snap, crunch! They munch in silence, side by side, in the soft glow of Gloop's tummy.","“Say, Gloop… If you swallow all the stars, it will be even darker. For everyone. And for you, too.” Gloop stops chewing. He had never thought of that."],["They turn towards the porthole. Outside, the sky is almost empty. “I have an idea,” says Nestor. “It makes me a little bit scared. We are going to look at the dark. Together. Just for a tiny moment.”","He takes Gloop's paw. It is soft, and it is trembling. His is trembling, too. Then he gently lays the blanket over the glowing tummy, just like blowing out a candle.","At first, you cannot see anything at all. Then, little by little, their eyes adjust. And look… the dark is not empty. It is blue, a deep, deep blue, and as soft as velvet."],["“You know,” whispers Nestor, “you can only see the stars when it is dark. In the daytime, they are still there, but nobody looks at them.”","Gloop thinks very hard. Then he puffs himself up like a big balloon, opens the porthole, and blows. Whoooosh! A star shoots out of his mouth. Then ten. Then a thousand!","The sky lights up again, twinkling brighter than before. And inside the rocket: a red nightlight, a blue one, a little moon… Bip counts them one by one. “Seven. All present and correct.”"],["That evening, Nestor turns on only one nightlight. The little moon. The other six he gives to Gloop, to brighten up his cave on the nearby asteroid.","“What if I am still scared?” asks Gloop, his arms full of nightlights. “Then you look out of your window. I will leave my moon shining. You will know that I am here.”","Ever since, whenever you travel through that corner of space, you can spot two little lights waving to each other in the night. And all around them: stars, stars, stars."]]},"nestor_foret":{"t":"Nestor and the Little Wolf at the End of the Lane","sc":[["In Four Oaks Forest, people say an awful lot of things. They say the river tickles. They say the moon is a hazelnut. And they whisper that at the end of Pine Tree Lane, there lives a wolf.","“A wolf with teeth this big!” says Hazel the squirrel, stretching out her arms. “And yellow eyes!” chatters Margot the magpie. No one has ever seen him. But everyone knows.","Nestor listens along, munching on his snack. “Hmm... And is he mean?” “Of course he is!” they cry together. “He’s a wolf!”"],["One day, Nestor is heading home from his granny’s house, with a basket of honey bread tucked under his arm. He takes a little detour to pick some blackberries. Then another. And blackberry by blackberry, there he is... on Pine Tree Lane.","Here, the trees huddle close together. It is dark and shadowy. Behind a mossy trunk, two grey ears poke out. Then two eyes. Yellow ones.","Nestor freezes. The basket trembles in his paw. The grey ears tremble too. In fact, they look even more scared than he is."],["“You... you aren't going to shout, are you?” whispers a tiny voice. And from behind the pine tree steps a wolf pup. Tiny and scrawny, with paws far too big for him.","“Usually, they shout. Or they throw pine cones at me. My name is Loupiot.” His little nose twitches: it smells like honey bread. His tummy rumbles. Grrrouik!","Nestor looks at his teeth. They are small and sharp, it is true. But right now, they are only nibbling his lip, the way you do when you are trying not to cry."],["So Nestor thinks back to all the stories. Then he looks at what is right in front of him. And what he sees is someone who is hungry.","“Hmm. Do you like honey bread?” “I don't know. I've never tasted it.” Nestor sits down on a tree stump and breaks his slice in two. The bigger half he hands to Loupiot.","The little wolf savours every bite with his eyes closed, honey all over his whiskers. “It’s like eating sunshine,” he sighs. And his tail wags so hard that the pine needles swirl up all around."],["They chat all afternoon long. Loupiot knows heaps of clever things: how to tell which way the wind blows, how to walk without making a sound, and how to sing to the moon. “Awoooo!” “Hmmm-oooo?” tries Nestor. They burst out giggling.","“Why don't you ever come to the clearing?” Loupiot’s ears droop. “I went there once. Everyone ran away. So I just watch from far away. You always look like you're having such fun.”","Nestor feels a tug at his heart. He places a big paw on Loupiot’s shoulder. “Tomorrow, you're coming with me.”"],["The next day, when they arrive at the clearing, it is total chaos. “THE WOLF!” Hazel scampers right to the top of her tree. Margot flaps away into the sky. The rabbits dive into their burrows, leaving only two white tails sticking out.","Loupiot flattens his ears. “You see...” he whispers. He is already turning back, his tail tucked down. But a big paw stops him.","Nestor does not like speaking in front of everyone. His voice wobbles a little, but he stands up tall. “Have you ever seen him bite? No. Have you ever spoken to him? No, you haven't. Well, I have. His name is Loupiot. And he is my friend.”"],["A long silence. Then a pink nose peeks out of a burrow. It is Pompon, the littlest rabbit of all, the one who has not yet learned to be afraid.","He hops closer and closer. Loupiot flattens himself in the grass so he doesn't look so big. Pompon touches the tip of his nose. “It's all wet!” And the baby bunny rolls onto his back, giggling happily.","Then Hazel climbs down one branch. Then two. Margot lands on the grass, not too close just yet. “Ahem,” she coughs. “Can he play tag, this wolf of yours?” “I can play WOLF!” replies Loupiot."],["Never before had there been so much running and laughing in the clearing. Loupiot wins every single round, so they decide he must count to twenty instead of ten. He thinks that is very fair indeed.","That evening, there is honey bread for everyone, and Loupiot gets the very first piece. Nestor brought two whole baskets. He had planned ahead.","In Four Oaks Forest, people still say an awful lot of things. But now, when an “Awoooo!” echoes from the end of the lane, everyone says: “Listen! That's Loupiot calling us to play.”"]]},"nestor_ocean":{"t":"Nestor and Ondine's Song","sc":[["All summer long, Nestor had a friend unlike any other: Ondine. She was as big as an island, as blue as the evening sky, and whenever she laughed, she made great big waves.","Every morning, Nestor rowed out to sea in his little wooden boat. He tapped three times on the water: splash, splash, splash! And Ondine rose up from the deep, slow and dripping wet.","She carried him on her back and blew fountains high in the air. And in the evening, as the first stars twinkled, she sang: a slow, deep song that rocked the whole sea to sleep."],["But one morning, the air smelled different. High up in the sky, birds were flying past, all heading the very same way. And the water felt colder against his paws than yesterday.","Ondine gazed at the horizon. “Little bear,” she whispered, “it is almost time. When the water turns cold, the whales must travel south. Far, far away. It has always been this way.”","“Leave?” stammered Nestor. “B-but… are you coming back tonight?” “I shall return in the spring.”"],["Spring! That was at least a thousand sleeps away. A big, tight lump rose in Nestor's throat. And because he did not know what to do with it, the lump turned into a giant sulk.","“Then you're not my real friend!” he grumbled, folding his arms. “Real friends stay!” And he rowed back to the beach without looking behind him.","That evening, Ondine sang all the same. Nestor buried his head under his pillow. But he could hear every single note."],["The next day, Nestor had a plan. Down to the shore, he dragged the longest rope in the whole world. He tied one end to Ondine's tail, and the other to a big rock on the beach.","“There. Now you have to stay,” he declared, putting his hands on his hips. Ondine did not pull on the rope. She could have snapped it in an instant. Instead, she sighed, and her breath made a soft little cloud.","“Little bear. If I stay, the sea will freeze all around me. And far away, in the warm waters, a baby whale is waiting to be born. My baby.”"],["Nestor looked at the rope. He looked at his friend. And he looked at the sea, far, far away, where the water was warm.","Slowly, his paws untied the knot. It was the hardest knot he had ever undone, even though it wasn't tight at all.","“I don't want you to go,” he sniffled, pressing his cheek against his great big friend. And this time he cried for real, without any sulking. Ondine let him cry. She stayed right beside him, until the very last tear."],["“I shall share a whale secret with you,” she whispered. “My song travels through the water. Far, far away. Farther than any boat can sail. If you listen carefully, you will hear me.”","“Hmm… What about me? Will you hear me?” “If you sing.” So, all through the afternoon, Ondine taught him her song. Mmmm-oooo… Nestor sang terribly out of tune, so out of tune that the fish pulled funny faces. Ondine thought it was perfect.","He gave her his blue scarf, tying it gently to the tip of her flipper. In return, she gave him a round, smooth seashell. “For whenever you think of me.”"],["In the morning, all the whales gathered out at sea. It was time. Ondine blew one last fountain, the highest of them all, with a bright rainbow dancing inside.","Standing on tiptoe, Nestor waved his paw until the great blue tail was just a tiny speck. Then nothing. The sea was as flat as a mirror.","His heart felt heavy, clutching the seashell close to his chest. But it was no longer a hard lump. It felt soft and warm, just like a cosy blanket."],["The winter was long. Bundled up in his woolly hat, Nestor gathered shells to show Ondine. He practised his singing. Every night, he counted the sleeps, until he stopped counting at all.","On some evenings, he walked down to the jetty and pressed his ear right against the water. And there, from far, far away, deep down below… Mmmm-oooo. Two voices. One deep and low. And one tiny, brand-new voice.","Then, standing at the very end of the jetty, Nestor sang his answer back, dreadfully out of tune and wonderfully loud. Then he went off to bed with a smile. Spring always arrives in the end."]]},"nestor_noel":{"t":"Nestor and the Christmas He Couldn't Miss","sc":[["In winter, bears sleep. That is just how it is. As soon as the first snowflakes fall, Nestor's eyelids grow heavy, so very heavy, an enormous yawn escapes him, and off he goes, snoring right through until spring.","That is why Nestor has never seen Christmas. Not once! Every year, waving their paws, his friends tell him all about it: the twinkling tree, the candles, the carols, and the spicy cinnamon cake.","“This year,” Nestor announces, raising a paw, “I will not sleep. I cross my heart and swear on my honey pot. I want to see Christmas too!”"],["To stay awake, he tries everything. A handful of snow down his neck: brrr, it makes him shiver! He counts the snowflakes, he sings at the top of his voice. And yet he still drifts off, standing straight up, right in the middle of the chorus.","Rosie tugs his paw to gallop him round the clearing. Filou tells him jokes. Plume asks a thousand questions. “Nestor? Nestor!” “Eh? I wasn’t sleeping,” he mumbles. “I was just thinking with my eyes closed.”","Only three days left until Christmas. Nestor is hanging on tight. His eyes are as red as redcurrants, he ties his scarf back to front, but he balls his paws into fists and stays awake."],["He helps carry the big Christmas tree. Up on his tiptoes, he hangs golden pinecones at the very top, way up high where nobody else can reach.","He tastes the cinnamon cake batter. Once, twice, three times, just to be sure. “So that’s what Christmas smells like,” he marvels, his snout completely smeared. Then he lets out an enormous, jaw-cracking yawn.","On Christmas Eve, he sits down for a minute by the crackling fire. Just one little minute. Just long enough to warm his paws."],["“Zzz-snore…” His friends shake him like an apple tree. Rosie tugs his ear. Filou blasts on his trumpet. Plume tickles his nose with the tip of a feather. No use at all! A sleeping bear is like a mountain.","“He’s going to miss everything,” Rosie groans. “He’ll be so upset!” The three of them gaze at their big friend, smiling in his dreams, his snout still dusted with cinnamon.","“Well then,” Plume whispers, lifting a wing, “if Nestor can’t come to Christmas…”"],["Together, the three of them heave him onto a sledge. Oof! They bump and slide him all the way to his den, then tuck him up under three cosy blankets. Nestor doesn't even twitch an ear.","Then Christmas arrives. The tree twinkles, the candles dance, carols drift into the night. It is lovely. But at the table, there is one big empty chair, and everyone keeps peeking at it out of the corner of their eye.","After the cake, Filou jumps up. “I’ve got an idea.” Rosie leaps up too. “I’ve got the very same one!” As for Plume, she is already tying her scarf."],["That night, on tiptoe, they creep into the den. Beside the bed, they set up a little tree. Right at the top, they hang a star. And under his pillow, they hide three parcels.","And on a plate, they leave the biggest slice of cinnamon cake. Wrapped up snugly in a tea towel, so it keeps just right.","In his sleep, Nestor twitches the tip of his nose. “Mmm… Christmas…” he mutters. Then off he goes back to sleep, while the snow falls softly and silently over the forest."],["One morning, a warm sunbeam tickles his ear. Birds are chirping. The snow has melted away! Nestor peeks open one eye… and leaps to his feet! “CHRISTMAS! I missed Christmas!”","A big lump rises in his throat. But then he spots the little tree. The star. The three parcels. And the slice of cake, a little dry now, but still smelling sweetly of cinnamon.","In the first parcel, a striped scarf. In the second, a drawing of the four of them. In the third, an enormous alarm clock, with a note scribbled by Filou: “For next year.”"],["Nestor steps out of his den, his heart thumping. In the clearing carpeted with primroses, his friends are waiting for him. They have put on their red hats again and relit all the candles.","“Merry Christmas, Nestor!” They sing all the carols, a tiny bit out of tune, under the blossoming trees, and Nestor taps along to the beat. It is the only Christmas in the whole wide world with butterflies fluttering by.","Nestor savours his slice of cake, his eyes closed, his friends snuggled close beside him. “Mmm… So that’s what Christmas is,” he whispers. “I thought it was just a day. But it’s really about friends.”"]]},"nestor_halloween":{"t":"Nestor and the Creaky House","sc":[["On Halloween evening, an idea pops into Filou's head. “What if we go inside the crooked old house at the top of the hill? The creaky one. People say it's haunted!”","“I dare you!” says Rosie. “Why not,” Plume replies. Everyone turns to look at Nestor, dressed up as a pumpkin. His tummy ties itself in knots. “Um… yes, yes,” he stammers with a tiny smile. “Not scared at all.”","That is not true at all. Just looking at the house makes Nestor's throat feel tight. But nobody else seems to be frightened. So he stays quiet."],["The door groans. Creeeeak! Inside, it is pitch-black, it smells of dust, and cobwebs hang from the ceiling, as big as bedsheets.","“Not scared at all,” Filou claims, his voice a bit too squeaky. “Not scared at all,” Rosie whispers, walking right beside him. “Not scared at all,” Nestor repeats. His knees are knocking like castanets.","They creep down the hallway. Every floorboard creaks. Creak. Creak. And upstairs, something answers back. Thud!"],["“It's just the wind,” Plume says reassuringly. Thud, thud. “It's a shutter,” Rosie whispers. Then a long moan drifts down the stairs. Woooooo…","“Shall we go up?” Filou asks, biting his lip. He does not look terribly keen. But it was his idea. And Rosie accepted the dare. You can never back down from a dare.","Nestor stares at the stairs, dark as a deep well. His heart thumps. His paws are freezing, and the lantern wobbles. His whole body is telling him one thing, and it definitely isn't “not scared at all.”"],["He puts one paw on the first step, then stops. His thoughts spin round and round: “If I say it, they will laugh at me. They will say I'm a baby.”","Then another thought pops into his head: “But if I don't say it, we'll have to go up. And I don't want to go up at all.”","So Nestor takes a deep breath, and he says, quite softly: “I'm scared. I'm too scared. I'm not going up. I'll wait for you outside.”"],["Complete silence. Nestor waits for them to laugh. With his snout down, he stares at his pumpkin feet.","“Phew,” whispers a little voice. “I'm scared too.” It is Rosie. Rosie! “I've been scared since the front door,” Plume admits. “And I've been scared since the bottom of the hill,” Filou mutters.","All four of them look at one another. Then they burst out laughing, a big, belly-aching laugh that sends the dust flying. It feels so good that the house already seems much less dark."],["Woooooo… The moan starts again. But now that they are not pretending anymore, they listen more closely. “It sounds like… someone crying,” Plume notices.","At the top of the stairs, a tiny shadow appears, hanging upside down. It is a little bat, no bigger than a pine cone. “Are you leaving already?” she sniffs, wiping away a tear.","“My name is Vesper. I live here. I got everything ready, the sweets, the paper chains… but nobody ever comes up. Everyone is too scared of my house.”"],["Nestor looks up at the stairs. It is still dark up there. He is still a little bit scared. “If we go up together,” he suggests, “and I can come back down whenever I want… then I will.”","They climb the stairs in single file, paw in paw. The thud-thud was just Vesper setting up her table! Waiting up there for them are glowing candles, garlands of autumn leaves, and a big bowl full of sweets.","“You are my very first guests!” chirps Vesper, glowing with delight. It is the best Halloween tea party in the whole forest. Right inside the scariest house."],["On the way back home, Filou nudges Nestor gently. “I'm so glad you said something. I would have gone upstairs with my teeth chattering, without telling a soul.”","“Nestor is the bravest of all!” Rosie declares, lifting his paw high. “He said the hardest thing.” Nestor blushes under his pumpkin costume.","Ever since that night, whenever something frightens them too much, the four friends have a secret password. They never say “not scared at all” anymore. Instead, they say: “A little bit. How about you?”"]]},"nestor_ski":{"t":"Nestor and the Great Sledge Race","sc":[["Today, at White Pines Resort, it is the great sledge race! The first one down to the bottom wins a chocolate medal as big as a dinner plate.","Nestor dreams of winning it. Usually, he always comes dead last: too slow to run, too heavy to climb. But on a sledge, the heavier you are, the faster you go! He pats his big, round tummy.","“For once,” he thinks, gripping his rope, “I'm going to win!” Mr Cabri puts the whistle to his lips. Tweeeet!"],["Nestor’s sledge begins to glide forward. Then it picks up speed. Faster. Faster! He zooms past Plume. He zooms past Filou. He even zooms past Rosie, who can hardly believe her eyes!","“Wheeeee!” The wind pins his ears right back. The pine trees rush by in a blur. Nestor is in the lead. First place! Never in his whole life has this happened before.","Far below, he can already spot the finish line between two red flags, and the medal gleaming in the sun."],["Just then, a tiny sound reaches his ears from the side of the track. A squeaky little cry. “Help…”","In a deep snowdrift, a sledge is overturned, runners sticking up in the air. And underneath, two little paws are wriggling. It’s Pip the hedgehog! He missed the bend.","Nestor is flying like the wind. In one second, he’ll have zoomed right past. Behind him, the others are catching up. “Someone else is sure to stop,” he tells himself."],["But the others are still far behind. And Pip is so very little, stuck in that freezing hole. Nestor looks at the medal. He looks at the tiny paws.","Then he digs both heels deep into the snow. Skrrrrrtch! His sledge skids to a halt in a great white cloud. Rosie whooshes past. Then Filou. Then Plume.","“Oh, bother,” sighs Nestor. Then he trudges back up the slope, puffing and panting, knee-deep in the snow."],["He lifts the little sledge. Underneath, Pip is as white as a snowman, teeth chattering. “I'm… I'm… so cold,” he shivers. “And I’ve lost my mitten.”","Nestor scoops him up from the snowy hole and rubs him warm. Then he tucks him inside his coat, snug against his big, cosy tummy. There is no better radiator than a bear cub!","“Did you stop your race for me?” asks Pip, wide-eyed. Nestor shrugs his shoulders. “Hmm… It wasn't that important.” That isn't quite true. But as soon as he says it, it feels true."],["Pip’s sledge is smashed to pieces. So they both climb onto Nestor’s sledge together. Carrying one bear cub and one hedgehog, it glides peacefully down, in no hurry at all.","Tucked up warm, Pip gets his rosy cheeks back. Around every bend, he throws his arms in the air. “Again!” Nestor chuckles. Earlier on, he hadn’t noticed how lovely the mountain was, with all its sparkling pine trees.","When they finally reach the bottom, the race has been over for ages. Rosie is wearing the chocolate medal around her neck."],["“In last place!” announces the loudspeaker. “Nestor!” A few animals giggle. But then Pip pokes his head out from the coat and tells everyone the whole story. The hole. The cold. The heels dug into the snow.","Nobody giggles anymore. Rosie looks down at her medal. She slips it off her neck. Snap! She breaks it into pieces. “It’s much too big for just me,” she announces.","Everyone gets a piece. And the very biggest chunk, she hands to Nestor, whose cheeks turn pink. “You were the real winner,” she says. “I know it, because you overtook me!”"],["That evening in the chalet, everyone is sipping hot chocolate. Pip has curled up into a ball and fallen fast asleep on Nestor’s tummy. Nobody dares to move him. Especially not Nestor.","On the mantelpiece sits a brand-new medal. It is made of cardboard, cut out a little bit crookedly, and coloured in with crayons. Pip made it himself. On it, he drew a bear cub with a big heart.","You cannot eat it. Yet Nestor presses it gently to his chest. He will keep it forever."]]},"nestor_mer":{"t":"Nestor and the Yellow Rubber Ring","sc":[["At Dunes Beach, all of Nestor's friends are in the water. Filou dives, Rosie floats on her back, and Plume splashes about in the shallows. But Nestor stays sitting on his towel, with his big yellow rubber ring around his tummy.","“Come on in, Nestor! It’s lovely!” “Mmm… in a bit. I’m digesting.” He ate three hours ago.","The truth is, Nestor is scared of the water. It never stops moving, it’s deep, and you can’t see what’s hiding underneath. Besides, bear cubs sink like stones. He is sure of it."],["Even so, he creeps down to the water’s edge and dips a toe in. Brrr, that’s cold! A tiny ripple comes rushing in, no thicker than a pancake. Nestor jumps back ten whole paces.","“Just jump straight in!” Filou calls out. “Like me!” “Close your eyes and run!” adds Rosie. Nestor tries to run. But right at the water’s edge, his paws freeze all on their own, as if he has hit a wall.","He trudges back to sit down, his shoulders slouched. Out in the waves, his friends are laughing. Nestor feels very big, very heavy, and very alone on his towel."],["“It scares you, doesn’t it?” It is Mr Otto, the lifeguard seal, wearing his bright red swimming cap. Nestor looks down at his paws. “A bit. A lot. The others aren't scared.”","“Shall I tell you a secret? When I was little, I refused to put my head under the water. A seal! My mother was so embarrassed.” “And then?” “Then I learned. One whisker at a time.”","“You don’t jump into the sea all at once. You get to know it first. Come along. Today, we only wet our feet. And that’s all.”"],["Only his feet? Nestor can manage that. Clinging tightly to Mr Otto’s flipper, he wades into the water up to his ankles. The tiny waves tickle his toes. He giggles.","“That’s enough for today,” the seal announces. “Already?” “Already. Tomorrow, up to our knees.” Nestor walks back to his towel with his chin held high. He has only wet his feet, but he feels as proud as if he had swum the whole ocean.","The next day: knees. The day after that: tummy. The yellow rubber ring bobs around him like a big duck."],["On the fourth day, Mr Otto says, “Today, we blow bubbles.” Nestor dips his chin in. Then his mouth. Blblblblb! It bubbles away just like a kettle.","Suddenly, a wave washes right over his nose. He coughs, he splutters. His heart thumps fast. “I want to get out!” “Out we go,” Mr Otto replies calmly. “We’ll come back whenever you’re ready.”","Nestor sits on the sand, his nose prickling. He expects to be too frightened to ever go back. But after five minutes, he stands up all by himself, his brow furrowed. “Mmm… More bubbles.”"],["On the sixth day, Mr Otto teaches him the starfish float. “On your back, spread your paws, and let the sea carry you.” Nestor lies flat on the water, stiff as a board. “It’ll never hold me. I’m too heavy.”","“The sea carries whales, my boy.” Nestor lies back. The seal’s flippers gently hold him up. The water fills his ears. All he can see is the sky, bright blue, with a seagull gliding by.","“Shall I let go?” “… Yes.” The flippers slide away. And Nestor floats! All by himself. Like a big, furry starfish. Left behind on the sand, the yellow rubber ring watches on."],["On the last day of the holidays, Mr Otto takes three steps back into the water. “Come over to me. Any way you like.” Nestor puffs out his chest. He kicks with his paws.","He paddles, he splashes, he swallows a mouthful of sea. It isn’t very graceful. But he moves forward. One stroke. Two. Five! He grabs hold of the seal’s flipper. “I swam!”","On the beach, his friends cheer with joy. “NESTOR! NESTOR!” Filou does a cartwheel. Rosie jumps up and down. “Five strokes! I counted them!”"],["That afternoon, Nestor splashes in the waves with the others. Not too far out. Only where he can touch the bottom. But right in the water. And when a rolling wave knocks him over, he pops back up laughing, with water in his ears.","On the sand, a tiny little rabbit gazes at the sea, clutching his towel tight. It is Pompon. Nestor waddles out of the water and sits down beside him, dripping wet.","“It scares you, doesn’t it? It scared me too, before. Here.” He slips the big yellow rubber ring around Pompon's tummy. “Today, we only wet our feet. And that’s all.”"]]},"plume_pirates":{"t":"Plume and the Tiny Wee Voice","sc":[["On board The Seashell, when night falls, the whole crew goes to sleep. The whole crew except Plume, perched high up the mast with her lantern. An owl at night sees absolutely everything.","From up there, she sees the fish sparkling under the hull. She sees shooting stars dash across the sky. And she even sees the captain tiptoeing on her paws to gobble up the rest of the cake.","Plume sees it all. But she doesn't breathe a single word. Her voice is so tiny, so squeaky-small, that she worries nobody will hear her. Or worse: that they will hear her and laugh."],["By day, on deck, it's a contest to see who can shout the loudest. Captain Mirabelle thunders. The sailors bellow their sea shanties. Sardine squeaks. What a racket! You couldn't hear yourself sneeze.","Plume raises a wing. “Hoo... excuse me... I think the rope has come undone...” Nobody hears her. Flap! The sail drops right on top of the cook. All that's poking out is a snout and a ladle.","“You should have said something!” grumble the sailors. “I did say something...” murmurs Plume with her head down. But nobody hears that either."],["One night, the fog rolls in. Thick, grey and woolly. It swallows the sea, it swallows the stars. The sailors are snoring away. Plume hops up into the crow's nest and opens her big round eyes wide.","And there, in the middle of all that grey, she spots a shadow. A big black shadow, without a single lantern, gliding across the water without a sound. A boat!","Standing at the bow is a cat with a sneaky grin and a patch over one eye. Clawford! That cheeky scoundrel is sneaking over to pinch their treasure while everybody sleeps."],["Plume's heart beats like a little drum. Quick as a flash, she flutters down the mast to the captain's hammock. “Hoo... Captain... there's a...”, she whispers right in her ear.","“Snxxx... pheeew...” The captain rolls over without opening an eye. Plume tries with Nestor. “Hoo... please wake up...” The big bear smiles happily in his sleep. He's dreaming of honey.","On deck, Plume freezes. The black boat is very close now. So close that she can hear Clawford's claws scraping the wood. Skrrrrtch!"],["Plume trembles from her beak to her tail feathers. What can she do? Ring the big alarm bell? Far too heavy for her wings. Bang the drum? She hasn't got any drumsticks.","All she has left is her voice. Her tiny wee voice. The one nobody ever hears. Plume rests a wing against her throat. “I'll never manage,” she thinks.","A grey paw reaches over the ship's rail. Then another. And a yellow eye glints through the fog, as round as a shiny gold coin."],["So Plume flies right to the very top of the mast. She puffs out her feathers, she puffs out her chest. Then she thinks of all the words she never dared say, all the words stuck in her throat, and she gathers them all in her beak.","“HOOOOO-HOOOOO! THIEVES! HOOOOO-HOOOOO!” Her cry rolls through the fog just like a foghorn. Plume's eyes go wide: she had no idea she had all that inside her!","Crash, bang, wallop! The captain tumbles out of her hammock, paws in the air. Nestor leaps up. Sardine waves a fork. “ALL HANDS ON DECK! PIRATE ALERT!”"],["Clawford has never had such a fright in all his nine lives. A ghost is shrieking in the sails, he's sure of it! His claws slip and... splash! Away he swims back to his boat, soaked right down to his whiskers.","“Who raised the alarm?” wonders the captain, lifting her lantern. “What a voice! What a set of lungs!” Everyone looks around, shrugging their shoulders. And high up on the mast, a tiny owl shyly lifts a wing.","“It was me...” It's only a whisper. But this time, a great hush falls over the deck, and every single sailor hears."],["Ever since that night, Plume has been the official lookout on The Seashell. No need to shout: the moment she raises a wing, the crew stop singing straight away and prick up their ears.","“The rope is loose.” “There's a sandbank on the left.” “Captain, you've finished the cake again.” Mirabelle blushes, with crumbs all over her whiskers.","Her voice is still quiet and gentle. But nowadays, everyone leans in to listen. And on some moonlit nights, right at the top of the mast, she closes her eyes and lets out a big, happy “Hoo-hoo!” to no one at all. Just for fun."]]},"plume_dino":{"t":"Plume and the Thousand Whys","sc":[["In Fern Valley, Plume has a collection. Not pebbles, nor seashells. A collection of questions, which she gathers everywhere she goes.","“Why do ferns uncurl? Why does Tric have three horns and not four? Why does the volcano snore? And why do we say why?” Tric is left completely flabbergasted.","She asks a hundred every single day. At breakfast, at naptime, and even when Tric, curled up under his fern, would really rather sleep."],["“Enough with your whys!” groans Tric, covering his ears. “You're making my horns ache.” “Things are just the way they are,” grunts Grondar the tyrannosaurus. “End of story.”","“We’ve always done it this way,” the grown-ups reply with a shrug. That is the answer Plume likes least of all.","So one day, Plume decides to stop. She folds up her questions, packs them right at the back of her head, wraps herself in her wings, and shuts her beak. Snap!"],["The valley is very peaceful without all the whys. Tric has a snooze. Grondar does too. But Plume's head is tickling, just like your nose when you are holding back a sneeze.","The thing is, she notices some rather funny things. This morning, all the birds zoomed off towards the sea. Why? The frogs are leaving the pond in single file. Why?","And the river! Plume dips the tip of a wing in: the water is as warm as a bath. Why, why, why? The questions knock inside her head. Tap, tap, tap."],["Plume cannot hold it in any longer. With a flap of her wings, she flies to Granny Dipla, the oldest and wisest dinosaur in the valley. “Granny... I know I shouldn’t... but I’ve got three whys tickling me,” she confesses.","Granny Dipla lowers her long, long neck down to the rock. “Heh heh. Hand them over, sweetheart. It’s been ages since anyone asked me a proper question,” she chuckles.","Plume explains everything, flapping her wings wide. The birds. The frogs. The warm water. Granny Dipla listens without interrupting. And the more she listens, the further her glasses slide down her nose."],["“Warm water... The birds...” mutters the old lady. “My grandmother used to tell me about that, when I was only knee-high to a fern.” Slowly, she lifts her head towards the mountain.","The Big Snorer, the old volcano, is not snoring anymore. It is smoking. A plume of black smoke, rising straight into the sky. “It is going to wake up, my dear,” whispers Granny Dipla. “Tonight, perhaps.”","She nuzzles Plume with the tip of her snout. “Everyone saw the birds leave. But no one asked why. No one, except you.”"],["They must warn the whole valley! Granny Dipla is far too slow. So Plume swoops off, from fern to fern, right onto Tric’s head. “Wake up! The volcano! We have to leave!”","“You again?” yawns Tric, opening his jaws wide. “And why would we leave?” Plume stamps her feet between his horns. “Aha! You see, that’s a good question! Touch the river.”","Tric dips a paw in. Ouch, it’s hot! He looks at the smoke, he looks at Plume, and his eyes go round as saucers. “GRONDAR! Get up! Plume was right!”"],["The whole valley sets off towards Blue Hill. Grondar carries the eggs close to his chest, ever so gently, without cracking a single one. Granny Dipla has hoisted the little ones onto her back. And right out in front, Plume leads the way.","They reach the top just as the sun is setting. And then... BOOM! The Big Snorer spits a shower of sparks. The lava flows, red and slow, right where they were sleeping only this morning.","Nobody says a word. It is beautiful and terrifying all at once. Grondar sets the eggs down in the grass. Everyone is there, safe and sound. Not a single scale is missing."],["In the dark, a deep voice clears its throat. It is Grondar, feeling rather shy. “Tell me, Plume... Why does lava make light?”","“And why does smoke go up?” adds Tric. “And why don't the stars fall down?” squeaks a tiny dinosaur, nose pointed at the sky. Plume's eyes go wide with delight.","“I haven't the foggiest!” she cries, opening her wings wide. “But we are going to find out!” And that night, up on the hill, nobody sleeps a wink. There are far too many whys to share."]]},"plume_chevaliers":{"t":"Plume the Apprentice Magician","sc":[["Right at the top of Highrock Castle, in the leaning tower, lives Master Balthazar. He is an old raven wizard who wears a star-spangled hat, with pockets always full of crumbs.","Since Monday, he has had an apprentice: Plume. She has read all the big spellbooks and knows every magic rhyme right to the tips of her feathers. All she needs to do now is have a go.","“First spell,” announces Master Balthazar, “making a feather fly. It’s the easiest one.” Plume squeezes her eyes tightly shut and raises her wand. “Abracadabri, abracadabra!”"],["Poof! The feather does not fly. It turns into a frog. A big, bumpy green frog. “Croak,” grumbles the frog, not happy at all.","Plume tries again. Poof! This time, a cloud bursts right above her head: it’s raining soup! She tries again. Poof! Master Balthazar’s hat begins to sing. Terribly out of tune.","“Never mind,” says the old raven gently, dabbing the soup off his feathers. “Try again.” But Plume’s eyes are prickling with tears. In the books, it always worked on the very first try."],["On Tuesday, she gets it wrong nine times. The feather turns into a sausage, a cloud, and an old sock. On Wednesday, she gets it wrong seventeen times. And on Thursday, oh dear, she gets herself wrong: there she is, bright blue from head to claws!","Down in the courtyard, everyone starts to giggle. “Look, it’s raining soup! That little owl is at it again!” Sir Gontran, the peacock, even makes up a silly song and squawks it for all to hear.","That evening, Plume drops her wand on the table. “I’m no good at this,” she sniffs. “I’ll never manage it. Real magicians never make mistakes.”"],["That night, she packs her little bundle. Three books, a hazelnut, and that is all. Then she creeps down the spiral staircase, step by step, on the very tips of her toes.","But waiting on the bottom step is someone unexpected. It is Monday’s frog. “Croak. Before you run away, you ought to take a peek inside the big chest upstairs. The one Balthazar never shows to anyone.”","Plume hesitates. A secret chest? But why? She simply cannot resist: back up she creeps."],["The lid creaks open. Inside, there is no gold or jewels. Just notebooks. Dozens of old, dog-eared notebooks, covered in messy scribbles and messy splatters.","By the glow of her lantern, Plume reads: “Attempt number 1: failed. The feather caught fire. Attempt number 2: failed. Turned my teacher into a teapot. Attempt number 212: failed, but a bit better.”","She turns the pages. Failed. Failed. Failed. Then she closes the notebook. On the cover, written by a very little crow, is a name: Balthazar."],["“Ah. You found my treasures.” Master Balthazar is standing there in his nightshirt, resting a gentle wing on her shoulder. “Four hundred and three tries before I could make my first feather float. I kept every single mistake, like a treasure.”","“But… why keep all the mistakes?” The old raven hugs a notebook close to his heart. “Because they are what taught me everything. The burning feather taught me not to shout. The teapot taught me to speak clearly.”","He gives her a warm wink. “A magician who never makes a mistake doesn't exist, Plume. Or if they do, it means they have stopped trying new things.”"],["The next morning, Plume opens a brand-new notebook. In her neatest handwriting, she writes: “Attempt number 31.” A flick of her wand… Poof! A carrot. So she writes down: “Failed. Said abracadabri too fast.”","Attempt 32: the feather quivers. Attempt 40: it lifts by an inch, wobbles, and flops back down. Plume does not cry anymore. She takes notes, she thinks, and she tries again.","And then, one morning… “Abracadabri, abracadabra.” The feather lifts. Up, up it floats, twirling and dancing in a beam of golden sunlight, right before Master Balthazar’s eyes. She did it! It is flying!"],["Plume opens the window wide. The feather flutters out, followed by a hundred more, then a thousand more, swirling high above the castle like a flurry of summer snow.","Down below, nobody is teasing anymore. Everyone is watching, mouths wide open. Even Sir Gontran. Especially Sir Gontran, who is craning his neck to see where all the gorgeous feathers from his tail have gone!","That night, by candlelight, Plume writes in her notebook: “Attempt number 58: hooray!” She draws a neat little star. Then she adds, in tiny letters: “Tomorrow, I will try something I don't know how to do yet.”"]]},"plume_espace":{"t":"Plume and Zig, the Alien Who Spoke in Colours","sc":[["At Three Moons Station, a newcomer has just arrived. His name is Zig. His home planet is so far away, so lost in the depths of space, that it doesn't appear on any map at all.","Zig is green, squishy and wobbly like jelly. His three eyes, perched on three long stalks, look off in every direction. And best of all, Zig doesn't talk. Not a single word.","Instead, he lights up! Yellow, blue, pink, drifting across his body like little clouds. Plume has never seen anything quite so pretty."],["“Hello there!” calls Filou. “Do you want to play?” Zig turns bright yellow. “He’s not answering. He's weird,” grumbles the little fox. “Would you like a biscuit?” offers Nestor. Zig turns orange, with polka dots.","“He didn't even say thank you!” After a week, nobody talks to him any more. “Leave him be, he doesn't understand anything.” And they play ball without him.","Zig stays curled up in his corner, by the big porthole. He is a dull, gloomy blue-grey, the colour of a rainy day. Nobody wonders what it means. Nobody, except Plume."],["Plume isn't very chatty either. But she watches closely. And by watching, she has noticed something: Zig's colours never appear by chance.","When the ball rolls near him: yellow. When the air smells of delicious cake: spotted orange. And when the door slams shut… bang! Violet, all in a flash.","Plume opens her little notebook. Very neatly, she writes it down: “Yellow, happy? Orange, yummy? Violet, scared?” And she comes back the next day. And the day after that. And the day after that, too."],["One morning, she sits down right beside him, without making a sound. Zig turns a little bit violet. She doesn't move; she just waits. Very gently, the violet fades away.","Then Plume takes out her torch, slips a piece of yellow paper over the front, and switches it on. Click! A round beam of yellow light. Zig’s three eyes swivel towards her, all at the same time.","He looks at the torch, and then he looks at Plume. Suddenly, his whole body blazes with brilliant yellow, crackling with little sparks. Now that isn't in the notebook. But Plume understands, loud and clear."],["From then on, they chat every single day. Plume with her coloured papers, and Zig with his skin. Light blue: “How are you?” Pink: “I like you.” Blinking green: “Tickles!”","Zig teaches her colours she has never seen before. There is one, between blue and gold, that shimmers whenever he gazes at the stars. It means: “I miss my home.” Plume thinks it is ever so beautiful, and ever so sad.","She answers him in pink. Zig snuggles close to her, right under her wing. He is warm, just like a hot-water bottle."],["One evening, snap! The whole station goes pitch black. No lights. No radio. A giant power cut! The little astronauts grope about, bumping into walls in the dark. “I'm scared!” “Where is the door?”","They call out and shout, but their voices get lost down the long corridors. Nobody knows which way to go.","And yet, deep in the dark, a soft green glow is pulsing. There is someone here who doesn't need a torch to make himself understood."],["Zig lights up. Bright! Brighter than a nightlight, brighter than a lighthouse beam. “Follow him!” calls Plume. “But we don't understand a word he's saying!” Filou cries. “I do.”","Zig turns green. “Green means: forward march!” translates Plume. Red. “Red means: stop, mind the step.” Swirling blue. “Turn left!” And the whole line follows the little light, in single file, holding paws.","They burst into the great hall just as the power hums back on. Everyone is there, safe and sound! Zig twinkles in every colour all at once, a magical swirl that even Plume has never seen. She guesses what it means: he is very, very proud."],["The next day, Filou arrives with a yellow paper stuck to his forehead. “Does this really mean hello?” he asks nervously. As for Nestor, he has baked an orange cake with spots.","Now, at Three Moons Station, everyone speaks a little bit of Zig. And Zig has learned a word of his own. Just one. He whispers it ever so softly, in a tiny bubble of a voice: “Plume.”","And whenever he says it, he turns a lovely shade of pink."]]},"plume_foret":{"t":"Plume, Hazel and the Treehouse Split in Two","sc":[["In Four Oaks Forest, Plume the owl and Hazel the squirrel have always been friends. Ever since long before one learned to fly, and the other to climb.","This summer, they have a grand project: to build the finest treehouse in the forest, right up high in the old oak tree. Hazel scrambles up, her arms full of branches. Plume draws the plans.","From morning till night, they are busy as bees. They even have a secret password: “Carabistouille!” Just saying it makes them giggle and chuckle until their tummies ache."],["One day, the treehouse is almost finished. All it needs now is the flag. “We’re putting up a red leaf,” Plume decides. “The treehouse was my idea.”","“Your idea?” Hazel gasps. “I carried all the branches! We’re putting up a jay’s feather, blue as the sky.” “My idea!” “My branches!”","Each pulls from her own side. Crack! The stick snaps clean in two. “You’re not my friend anymore!” “Neither are you!” The words tumble out so fast they hang in the air between them, completely astonished."],["Hazel draws a white line on the floor, right down the middle of the treehouse. “That is my side.” “Fine,” Plume replies, crossing her wings. “And that is mine. You’re not allowed to look out of my window!”","There they stand, beak to nose, each on her own side, without a word. Plume has the plans, but no more branches. Hazel has the branches, but no more plans.","On the first day, they sulk splendidly, chins held high, cheeks puffed out. It’s actually rather nice, sulking. At first."],["On the second day, Plume spots a snail wearing a mushroom, just like a little hat. Quickly, she turns round to tell Hazel… Oh no. That’s right.","On the other side of the line, Hazel finds a hazelnut shaped like a heart. She half turns, opens her mouth… and closes it again.","It’s funny how much you have to tell someone, when you’re not speaking to them anymore."],["“She should say sorry first,” Plume grumbles at her window. “She was the one who shouted first.” “She should say sorry first,” Hazel grumbles at hers. “She started it.”","Old Mathurine the tortoise ambles by, taking tiny little steps. She looks up. “Well, well! Two half-treehouses. And two half-smiles. What a shame, they used to make a whole.”","Plume pretends not to hear. But she thinks about it all night long, her eyes wide open."],["On the third day, it begins to rain. Heavy summer rain, drumming on the leaves. The roof is only half-finished on each side. Drip, drop, it trickles onto Plume. Drip, drop, it trickles onto Hazel.","Plume shivers. At her feet, the white line is melting into little puddles: the rain is washing it away. Then she looks at Hazel’s back, all wet, all small.","Saying sorry first is like jumping into cold water. You’re scared beforehand. Plume takes a deep breath and reaches out a wing. “Hazel… Carabistouille?”"],["Hazel whips round with a leap. Her whiskers quiver. “Carabistouille,” she whispers back. And just like that, they burst out laughing, with a few tears too, but in the rain nobody can tell.","“I’m sorry. They were your branches.” “I’m sorry. It was your idea.” Forehead to forehead, they whisper together: “It was our treehouse.”","Hazel holds the branches. Plume points out where to put them. In ten minutes, the roof is finished. Not a single drop gets through: they are snug, dry, and safe."],["When the sun returns, bringing a rainbow with it, they hoist the flag. A red leaf and a jay’s feather, tied together. It looks much prettier.","Nothing is left of the line on the floor. Just a tiny mark in that very spot, which no one else would ever notice. But they do.","They still bicker, of course. Over a bilberry, over a song, over who spotted the snail first. But never for three days. Three days is far too long without saying “Carabistouille”."]]},"plume_ocean":{"t":"Plume and the Grey Reef","sc":[["Every summer, Plume slips on her little glass helmet and dives down to see her friends at Thousand Bubbles Reef. Of all the places in the world, it is the most colourful.","There are pink corals, waving orange anemones, and fish striped just like sweets. And tiny Pearl the seahorse, who guides her all around.","But this summer, when Plume arrives, something is wrong. She looks around, squinting her eyes. Why is that corner so pale? Suddenly she realises: the colours are missing."],["A whole corner of the reef has turned grey, dull as ash. Trapped among the corals are things that do not grow in the sea. A bottle. A tin can. An old shoe.","“It tumbles down from up above,” sighs Pearl. “More and more every day. The clownfish packed their bags and moved away. And the anemone won't open anymore.”","A bag floats in the water, bobbing about like a jellyfish. But it isn't a jellyfish. A little turtle swims up, feeling hungry, and opens her beak to take a bite…"],["“No!” Plume swoops in and snatches the bag just in time. The little turtle blinks, very surprised. “Isn't that food?” “Oh no. Not at all.”","Plume looks at the bag at the tip of her wing. Then at the reef. There is rubbish everywhere, as far as the eye can see. Hundreds of bits.","“We have to clear it all away!” she decides, spreading her wings wide. The fish look at each other, then swim backwards. “All of it? But that's impossible. We're too small. And there's far too much.”"],["Even Octave, the octopus with spectacles, shakes his head. “I tried, with all eight of my arms. The next day, just as much fell down again. What's the point?” he sighs.","Plume doesn't know what to say. It is true that she is small. It is true that there is far too much. Thoughtfully, she looks down at the tin can right by her feet.","So she picks it up and slips it into her net. “There,” she announces quietly. “That's one less.”"],["She picks up another. And another. All morning, without giving up, she fills her net while the fish watch. “She'll never manage it,” they whisper behind their fins.","By midday, Plume has cleared a patch no bigger than a dinner plate. A dinner plate is very small. But right in the middle, a coral has got a little pink back in its cheeks.","Pearl swims over. Tiny as she is, she can only carry a bottle top. But she carries it anyway, bravely. “Two less,” she declares."],["The little turtle comes to lend a flipper. Then a crab, carrying tin cans on his shell. Then two, then ten. “Well,” grumbles Octave, grabbing a bottle. “Eight arms doing nothing is a terrible waste.”","The fish make a chain, fin to fin. The prawns do the sorting. The crabs do the carrying. Even the grumpy old moray eel, who likes nobody, pokes her nose out of her hole to nudge a shoe along.","No one sighs, “We're too small,” anymore. There is no time for that! They count with their claws in the air: “A hundred less! Two hundred less!”"],["In the evening, Plume swims back up to the surface, dragging her bursting net behind her. On the beach, a big walrus in a striped swimsuit is finishing his picnic. There he is, lifting his empty bottle, ready to toss it into the waves.","Plume doesn't shout. She places the net right in front of him, and she tells him everything. The turtle. The anemone. The grey coral. The walrus looks down at his bottle. His big cheeks turn bright red.","“I didn't know it went anywhere,” he mumbles, looking very sheepish. And he takes it home with him. The next day, on the beach, someone has put up a big bin. With a fish painted on it."],["The following summer, when Plume puts on her helmet and dives, her heart beats a little fast. What if everything has turned grey again? Down at the bottom, Pearl and Octave are already waiting for her.","But Thousand Bubbles Reef has got its pinks back, its oranges, and its sweet-shop stripes. The clownfish have moved back in. And the anemone blooms wide, as if to say hello.","Now and then, a bottle still tumbles down. But whoever spots it first zooms off to pick it up. “One less!” they sing out. It has become the motto of the reef."]]},"plume_noel":{"t":"Plume and the Dark Window","sc":[["On Christmas Eve, the whole of Four Oaks Forest is sparkling. Lanterns sway gently in the trees, candles glow in the windows, and holly garlands dress the doors.","Plume glides over the rooftops, counting the lights. One hundred and twelve, one hundred and thirteen… And there, by the frozen pond, stands a house that is completely dark. Not a single candle. Not a single garland.","It is the home of Mr Grumbles, the old badger. “Don’t go there,” warns Filou, shaking his head. “He hates Christmas. He hates everyone. He even yelled at me, just for a tiny snowball!”"],["But Plume has a question tickling her thoughts. “Why? Why would anyone hate Christmas?” She lands on the doormat and knocks. Knock, knock.","The door creeps open just a crack. An eye gleams behind a pair of spectacles. “No carols! No biscuits! No merry this-and-that! Shoo!” And slam! Plume jumps, her feathers all ruffled.","Thoughtfully, she stays on the doormat. Behind him, on the mantelpiece, she had just enough time to spot something. A picture frame. With two badgers inside."],["Plume knows very well that you shouldn't peek through windows. She peeks anyway. Just a little peek.","Mr Grumbles is sitting in his armchair, quite still, his paws resting on his knees. Opposite him sits a second armchair, empty, with some knitting folded on it. He isn't reading. He isn't doing anything at all. He is just gazing at the frame.","Then Plume understands. He doesn't hate Christmas. Christmas simply reminds him of someone who isn't here anymore. And shouting is easier than crying."],["She tells her friends all about it. “We could sing him a carol outside his window!” cries Rosie. “Really loudly!” “And bring him an enormous cake!” adds Nestor.","Plume gently shakes her head. “He said no carols, no biscuits. We have to listen to him.” She closes her eyes and thinks for a long time. Plume's best ideas are often very little ones.","She chooses a lantern. Just one. She lights it, then carries it over to place it on the dark windowsill. Without knocking. Without a word. And she flutters away into the snow."],["Hidden behind a fir tree, she watches. The door opens. Mr Grumbles discovers the lantern. He grunts, peering left and right. Nobody.","He puffs out his cheeks to blow it out… and stops. The tiny flame dances in his spectacles. So he stands there for a long time in the cold, watching it flicker.","Then, ever so gently, he picks it up and carries it inside. Through the window, Plume sees him place it on the mantelpiece. Right beside the frame."],["On Christmas night, Plume comes back. This time, the door opens before she can even knock. “You're the lantern.” “Yes, sir.” “Hmph. You'll catch a chill,” he grumbles. “Come in. But no carols.”","Inside, it smells of dust and herbal tea. Mr Grumbles pours her a steaming cup. They don't talk. Plume is very good at saying nothing at all. In fact, it is her speciality.","After a while, he is the one who speaks. “Her name was Hortense. She used to put candles everywhere. Even in the bathtub.” And he chuckles, a rusty little laugh that hadn't been used in a very long time."],["He gets up and lifts the lid of an old chest. Asleep inside are dozens of candles and a dried-up holly garland. “She would have said my house is scaring the stars away.”","Together, they light the candles. One in every window. One on every stair step. And one in the bathtub, of course, because that was the tradition.","On the other side of the pond, the woodland folk look up. “Look! Over at Mr Grumbles' house!” The ice is glowing with light. One hundred and fourteen lights. Every single one is counted."],["Filou, Rosie and Nestor arrive on tiptoe, carrying a humble little cake. Mr Grumbles frowns. “I did warn you: no biscuits.” “It's a cake, sir.” “Hmph. Well, that's alright then.”","They snuggle close by the fire, huddled together on the rug. The second armchair stays empty, with its knitting. Nobody sits there. But it looks less lonely now, with all these little friends around.","“Right then,” grunts Mr Grumbles. “One carol. Just one. And not too loud.” They sing ever so softly, like a lullaby. And when they finish, he is the one who asks, “Again.”"]]},"plume_halloween":{"t":"Plume and the Biggest Pumpkin","sc":[["In the spring, Plume and Hazel each plant a pumpkin seed, side by side in the fresh, cool soil. It is for the big Halloween contest.","Plume does everything by the book. She waters in the morning, talks to her shoot in the evening, and measures her pumpkin every day with a bit of string.","Then autumn arrives. Plume’s pumpkin is as small as an apple. Hazel’s is as big as a wheelbarrow! “Why is that?” wonders Plume."],["Everyone comes running to admire Hazel’s pumpkin. “What a beauty!” “She’s going to win, for sure!” Hazel hops with joy.","Plume whispers, “Well done.” But at the very same moment, something tightens in her tummy. A little pinch. Cold, sharp, and a little bit green.","The pinch whispers in her ear. “It’s not fair. She didn’t even read the books. It’s just pure luck, that’s all. You deserve to win much more than her.”"],["Over the next few days, the pinch grows bigger. When Hazel comes over to play, Plume turns her back: she is busy. When Hazel laughs, Plume thinks she is laughing too loudly.","“Are you cross?” asks Hazel gently. “No.” “Are you sure?” “I said no!” It isn’t a real no. But how can she tell her best friend: I feel sad that you are happy?","The night before the contest, Plume cannot sleep. With a flap of her wings, she flits down to the vegetable patch. The big pumpkin glows softly under the moon."],["It sits right at the top of a little slope, held in place by a single stone. The pinch whispers: “If that stone moved… it would roll right into the stream. Splash! No one would ever know.”","Plume rests a claw on the stone. It is cold. Just one tiny push would be enough.","Then she thinks of Hazel, who hauled heavy watering cans all summer with her little paws. She thinks of Hazel’s face tomorrow morning, looking at an empty patch. Plume pulls her claw back with a jump, as if the stone were burning hot."],["She perches on the fence, trembling all over. “What was I about to do?” Inside, she feels awful. If only she could pluck out this pinch and throw it far, far away!","“Plume?” comes a little voice in the dark, and the flicker of a candle. It is Hazel in her nightie, who could not sleep either. “What are you doing out here?”","Plume could make up an excuse. Instead, she looks right at her friend, takes a deep breath, and tells the truth—the kind that is so hard to let out."],["“I’m jealous. Your pumpkin is enormous, and mine is ridiculous. I have a green pinch in my tummy, and it gives me horrible ideas. I’m sorry, Hazel.”","Hazel climbs up to sit beside her. For a moment, she stays quiet, looking up at the stars. Then she says, “Well, I get a pinch every time you fly away.”","“What?” gasps Plume. “You can fly. You can see the whole forest from above. I have to look up from down here, and I think: it’s not fair. I was always too shy to tell you.”"],["They look at each other. Two friends, two pinches. Suddenly it all seems rather silly, and they start to giggle softly, so as not to wake the slugs.","The next day, Hazel’s pumpkin wins first prize. Plume claps with both wings. The pinch is still there, all shrivelled up. But now that she has spoken it out loud, it is no longer the boss.","“What about yours?” Hazel cries. “We can’t leave it all alone!” Crouching down, they look at the apple-pumpkin. It really is tiny."],["Together, they scoop it out. They carve two eyes and a smile, and then pop a candle inside. It is the teeniest Halloween lantern in the whole forest.","And because it weighs almost nothing at all, Plume can carry it up into the air. “Hop on!” she calls. Hazel climbs onto her back, holds on with all her might, and peeks out.","That evening, everyone below sees a little orange star zooming over the treetops. And way up high, two voices laughing. As for the giant pumpkin, it stayed behind in the vegetable patch. It was far too heavy for an adventure like that."]]},"plume_ski":{"t":"Plume and Her First Night Away from the Nest","sc":[["For the very first time in her life, Plume is going on holiday without her parents. A whole week at White Pines Resort, with her friends, in a big wooden chalet.","In her suitcase, she has packed her hat, her mittens, and three books. And Raggy, her cuddly toy, she tucked right at the bottom so no one would see. She is a big girl now.","“You’ll see, it’s going to be wonderful,” Mum promises, hugging her tight. Plume nods. Then, from the back of the sledge, she waves her wing until Mum is just a tiny little speck."],["The day is wonderful. There is snow right up to the windows! They build a snowman, with a carrot for a beak. Then they whizz down the slope on their sledges, whooping with joy.","In the evening, they tuck into hot soup and melted cheese, and Mr Cabri tells the story of the yeti who was ticklish. Plume laughs so much that she gets the hiccups.","She doesn’t think about home even once. Not once. Until the lights are switched off."],["In the dormitory, her friends fall asleep straight away. Nestor snores. Rosie wiggles. Filou mumbles in his sleep. But Plume lies with her eyes wide open.","This bed doesn’t smell like her bed. The ceiling beams creak. There is no sound of Daddy tidying the kitchen, and no little ray of light peeking under the door.","A lump rises in her throat. It is big, it is warm, and it has a name Plume didn’t know yet: nest-sickness."],["She wants to go home. Right now. To fly all the way home, even through the night, even through the snow. But it is too far, and her wings are too small.","Very gently, she opens her suitcase a crack. She rummages under the mittens, pulls out Raggy, and hugs him tight. He smells of home. And then, the tears just start to flow.","She cries quietly, curled up beneath her blanket, so she won’t wake anyone. “I’m such a baby,” she thinks. “The others are fast asleep. I’m the only one who’s sad.”"],["“Plume?” comes a whisper from the bunk below. It’s Nestor. He isn’t snoring any more. “Are you crying?” Quickly, Plume hides Raggy under her blanket. “No. I just have... a cold.”","Silence. Then something climbs up over the edge of the bed. A worn-out bit of blue cloth, with a chewed-up ear. “Um... This is Bidou. I never sleep without him. Don’t tell Filou.”","Plume sniffs. She pulls Raggy back out. “This is Raggy.” In the dark, the two cuddly toys say hello, nose to nose. Things feel a tiny bit better already."],["The door creaks open. It’s Mr Cabri doing his rounds, carrying a lantern by his hoof. He spots their watery eyes straight away. He doesn’t say, “Come on, you’re big birds now.” He simply sits on the edge of the bed.","“The first night is always the hardest. You miss home.” “Why?” sniffs Plume. “Because you love it. That’s rather good news, isn’t it?” She hadn’t thought of it like that.","He pulls back the curtain. The moon is there, big and round, above the snowy pines. “Look at it closely. Your mummy can see it too, right now. It’s the very same moon. You’re looking at the exact same spot.”"],["Plume gazes at the moon with all her might. She pictures Mummy at the nest window, with her knitting. In her throat, the lump melts a little, like an ice cube in warm soup.","“Goodnight, Mummy,” she whispers. She drifts off to sleep, Raggy tucked under her wing. Down below, Nestor is already snoring, his nose buried in Bidou.","The next morning, she writes a postcard. “Dear Mummy, dear Daddy. I missed you very much last night. Today, I’m learning the snowplough. Lots of love, Plume.” And off she dashes outside."],["The second night is gentler. The third night too. Every evening, when the moon comes out, the lump returns a little. But now, Plume knows what to do.","At the end of the week, when the sledge brings her back, Mummy is waiting on the platform. Plume throws herself into her wings. “I missed you!” “I missed you too, my little owl. Every night, I looked at the moon.”","Plume’s eyes go wide. Then she unpacks her suitcase. This time, Raggy isn’t hidden at the bottom. He sits proudly right at the top, in plain sight. He went skiing too. And he has lots and lots of stories to tell."]]},"plume_mer":{"t":"Plume and the Red Kite","sc":[["It is the first day of the holidays at Dunes Beach. Plume does not know anyone. She has settled down in the shade of the parasol, with a book she isn't reading.","Because a little further away, three children are playing. A little otter, a seagull, and a crab. They are galloping after a bright red kite, laughing their heads off.","Plume peeks at them over her book. She would love to play with them. But how do you join in when a game has already started?"],["In her head, she practises what to say. “Hello, my name is Plume. Can I play with you?” She repeats it ten times. Twenty times. She knows it by heart.","She stands up and takes three steps. Her heart is thumping right up to her ears. “What if they say no? What if they laugh? What if they don't even hear me?”","She turns around and sits back down, feeling ever so glum. Her book is upside down. She does not even notice."],["On the second day, it is just the same. She edges a little closer. Right beside them, she gathers seashells, hoping they will notice her. They do not notice.","“Perhaps they’ll come and ask me,” she hopes. She waits. She builds a little mound of sand. She waits some more.","But they are totally wrapped up in their game. It isn't that they don't want her. They don't even know she is there."],["On the third day, the wind picks up. The red kite tugs and tugs at its string. And suddenly—snap! The string breaks. The kite swoops away over the dunes.","The three children dash after it. Up it goes, twirling round and round, until it gets stuck right at the top of the big pine tree, on the very highest branch.","“Oh no!” The otter jumps with her arms stretched high. The crab pinches the trunk. The little seagull flaps his wings, but he cannot fly yet. The kite is much too high."],["Under her parasol, Plume has not missed a thing. And she knows one thing: she can fly. In ten seconds flat, she could fetch it down.","But to do that, she would have to go over there. Right in front of them. Without her little speech, which has flown out of her head along with the kite. Her feet feel as heavy as two buckets of wet sand.","The little otter has sat down at the foot of the pine tree. Her chin is wobbling. Plume looks at that wobbly chin. And her feet lift up all on their own."],["Without a word, she runs, spreads her wings, and rises into the air. Higher than the parasol. Higher than the dune. Right up to the top branch of the big pine tree.","The kite is completely tangled. With the tip of her beak, Plume untangles the string, patiently, knot after knot. Down below, three little faces watch her, open-mouthed.","She glides back down, the red kite between her feet, and lands right in front of them. She must say something. Anything at all. “Here you are,” she stammers. “It… it was up there.”"],["“YOU CAN FLY!” chirps the little seagull at the top of his voice. “Did you see how she flies?” “What's your name?” asks the otter. “Plume.” “I'm Lila. This is Pinchy, and this is Gull-Who-Can't-Fly-Yet.”","“Want to play with us?” asks Lila. Just like that. Simply. As if it were the easiest thing in the world.","Plume thinks of her lovely sentence, practised twenty times. She didn't need it after all. Just a few words were enough, and she wasn't even the one who said them. “Yes,” she says."],["The rest of the holiday zooms by. They fly the kite higher than ever: Plume holds it, way up in the sky. She teaches the little seagull how to flap his wings. He lifts off by three whole feathers!","On the last evening, they swap seashells so they won't forget each other. “Are you coming back next summer?” “Promise.” Lila gives Plume hers, the prettiest of all, with a little hole to thread a string through.","On the train home, Plume hugs it close to her heart. At the station, an unfamiliar little owl is sitting on a bench, all by herself, watching the others play. Plume takes a deep breath. And off she goes."]]},"rosie_pirates":{"t":"Rosie, Captain for a Day","sc":[["Aboard The Seashell, nobody is faster than Rosie. She scampers up the mast in three hops, leaps over barrels, ties ten knots a minute, and is always finished before everyone else.","“If I were the captain,” she boasts, paws on her hips, “we would go twice as fast!”","One morning, Captain Mirabelle overhears her. Calmly, she takes off her big blue hat and places it over Rosie’s long ears. “You’re on! Today, you are in charge. As for me, I’m off to have a nap in my hammock.”"],["With a bound, Rosie perches on a barrel. “Listen up, crew! Set sail for Pineapple Island! Ready, steady, GO!”","“You, hoist the sail! Faster! You, scrub the deck! No, not like that! And you… give me that broom! Out of the way, I’ll do it myself!”","The crew bustles about, running and bumping into each other, buckets and ropes spilling over their paws. Rosie fires off thirty orders a minute. She chatters so quickly that she cannot hear anything besides her own voice."],["“Um… Captain Rosie,” starts Nestor, raising a paw, “the wind is turning, maybe we should…” “No time!” “Captain Rosie,” tries the cook, ladle in hand, “about lunch…” “Later!”","Right at the top of the mast, a tiny voice tries next. It is Sardine, the lookout mouse, leaning out of the crow’s nest. “Captain! I can see something, straight ahead!”","Rosie puts a paw to her lips. “Shh, Sardine! I am in charge, I know best! Faster with that sail!”"],["The Seashell cuts through the waves like an arrow. Rosie is thrilled. Right ahead, a big white cloud has settled over the sea, thick as cotton wool. Fog. “Straight through it! Full steam ahead!”","“Captain!” squeaks Sardine at the top of her voice. “In the fog, there are…” “I said shh!” Sardine opens her mouth. Closes it. Then she sits back down, ears drooping, and stays silent.","The fog swallows the boat completely. You cannot even see the tip of your snout, barely the faint glow of the lantern. Everything is white, everything is quiet. A little too quiet."],["CRUNCH! Everyone tumbles onto the deck, paws flailing in the air. The Seashell is wedged between two rocks, tilted like a plate stacked all wonky.","“Rocks!” gasps Rosie, her hat askew. “Why didn’t anyone warn me?” The crew stares at her without a word. Nobody answers.","Then Sardine raises a timid paw. Her voice is very tiny: “I did try, Captain. Twice.” Rosie feels her big ears turn very, very warm."],["Rosie slumps down onto the deck. The big hat slips over her eyes. It is heavy, after all. Much heavier than it looks.","She lifts the brim of the hat, wanting to bark an order. But which one? Her mouth opens, but nothing comes out. For the first time in her life, Rosie is completely speechless.","And in the silence, with one ear pricked up, she hears things. The creaking of the wood. The water lapping against the hull. And her crew whispering: “I’ve got an idea…” “Me too…”"],["Rosie takes off the hat and holds it between her paws. “I’m listening.” Saying those words feels very strange in her mouth. “Nestor?” “Well… If we all push on the same side, just as the wave comes in…”","“Sardine?” “From up here, I spotted a way through the rocks! I can guide you.” “And I,” adds the cook, waving a big block, “have butter to make the hull slide!”","A wave swells behind them. “All together!” calls Rosie. “One, two, three… PUSH!” They push, shoulder to shoulder. And whoosh-slide! The Seashell slips free, rights itself, and floats."],["“To the left!” shouts Sardine. Rosie turns left. “Straight on!” Rosie goes straight on. And suddenly, the fog parts like a curtain: there is the sun, and Pineapple Island, bright green and lovely.","In her hammock, Captain Mirabelle stretches just at that moment, one eye open, the other closed. Well… perhaps she wasn’t truly asleep. “So, Captain Rosie? How does it feel to be in charge?”","Rosie hands back her hat. “It takes a lot of listening,” she answers with a laugh. “Lucky I’ve got big ears!”"]]},"rosie_dino":{"t":"Rosie and the Moon Flowers","sc":[["In Fern Valley, Rosie does everything on the run. She eats on the run, she says hello on the run, and they even say she sleeps on the run! Behind her, all that is left is a little cloud of dust.","This morning, big news: right at the very top of Blue Hill, the moon flowers are going to open. They only bloom once a year, just as the sun goes down.","“I’ll be first! One, two, three...” “Hold your horses!” her mummy stops her. “You are going with Granny Dipla. She knows the way.” Rosie’s ears droop. “Oh no... Not Granny Dipla!”"],["Granny Dipla is the oldest diplodocus in the valley, as tall as a hill. She is ever so kind. And she is very, very, very slow.","She lifts one foot... puts it down... lifts the other foot... Meanwhile, Rosie has already galloped round a tree twelve times, making herself quite dizzy!","“Faster, Granny Dipla! We’ll miss everything!” “Heh heh... All in good time, my poppet. Oh, look at that beetle, how shiny it is!” Arms crossed, Rosie taps her foot. She does not look."],["After an hour, Rosie cannot stand it any longer. Just then, the path splits in two. “I’m taking the shortcut!” she calls, waving her paw. “See you up there!”","“Rosie, wait! That path...” But Rosie is already gone. Zoom! With her ears pinned back, she dashes off so fast that the ferns barely see her go by.","She jumps over a puddle. Hop! Two puddles. Hop! Then a third one, wider, browner, shinier. And that one... she doesn't jump quite far enough."],["SPLODGE! There goes Rosie, belly-deep in the mud. It is the Sticky Swamp, and it certainly lives up to its name! She tugs on one paw: stuck. On the other: even stickier!","“Ready, steady... go!” She wriggles, she wiggles, she paddles, splashing the frogs all around. But the more she hurries, the deeper she sinks.","Over yonder, the sun is starting to slip behind the hill. The moon flowers will open without her. And Rosie, the fastest little thing in all the valley, cannot budge an inch."],["Boom... Boom... A steady footstep makes the ferns tremble. Then a long, long neck bends over the swamp, with a pair of little shiny spectacles perched right at the end.","“There you are, my poppet.” Granny Dipla does not say “I told you so.” She lowers her big head, very gently, right down to Rosie’s paws. “Hold tight. And no wriggling.”","Rosie holds on tight. Granny Dipla lifts her, slowly, slowly. The mud goes slurp, then plop! And Rosie finds herself perched right up high, dripping wet, on the tallest back in the world."],["“Now, we go at my pace,” Granny Dipla announces. Rosie heaves a big sigh. But she is far too tired to run. So, for once, she looks around.","She spots a dragonfly drying its wings in the sunshine. Further away, the river weaves and winds, looping like a ribbon. And when Granny crushes the mint beneath her feet, Rosie closes her eyes to breathe in the sweet scent.","“Was all of this here before?” asks Rosie, lying flat on her tummy on the long neck. “Always, my poppet. But you cannot see it when you’re running.”"],["The sun is already skimming the hill when they reach the top. Rosie tiptoes as high as she can, her heart thumping. “Are we late? Have we missed it all?”","“Hush,” whispers Granny Dipla. “Sit down here. Now, you’ll have to do the hardest thing in the world.” “Run?” “No, my poppet. Wait.”","Rosie sits down. Her paws are tingling, itching to go. She closes her eyes and counts in her head: one... two... three... And she stays right there."],["Then, one by one, without any rush, the moon flowers unfold their petals. They are white, they are blue, glowing in the dusk as if each one had swallowed a tiny star.","Rosie does not twitch a single whisker. Not a word. She watches right until the very last flower, which blooms right in front of her nose. It takes time. It is wonderful.","On the way home, under the stars, she walks alongside Granny Dipla. One foot... then the other... “Not running, my poppet?” “Not tonight,” whispers Rosie. “Tonight, I don't want to get there.”"]]},"rosie_chevaliers":{"t":"Rosie and the Squires' Race","sc":[["At Highrock Castle, the grand squires' race takes place every year. And every year, Rosie wins, paws down!","Three golden cups sit proudly on her shelf. Every evening by candlelight, she polishes them until they sparkle. She has even measured out the space for the fourth one.","“Winning is easy,” she tells anyone who will listen, her nose in the air. “You just have to be me!”"],["This year, though, there is a newcomer on the starting line. It is Pip, the little hedgehog. He has tiny paws, scruffy prickles, and barely reaches Rosie’s knees.","“Are you going to run?” Rosie giggles behind her paw. “I am going to try,” Pip answers bravely. “I trained all winter long.”","Queen Hermine raises her white handkerchief. Hush... “One... two... three... GO!”"],["Rosie shoots forward like an arrow. She is soon so far ahead that she takes time to wave to the crowd. She even does a little twirl. “Look at me!”","But on the big downhill slope, a funny noise tickles her ears. Whirrrrrr... Pip has rolled into a ball. And a ball on a slope... rolls!","Whirrrrr-ZOOM! The prickly ball zooms right past her, darts across the little stone bridge, and crosses the finish line first. By a tiny hedgehog nose!"],["“The winner is Pip!” The crowd bursts into cheers. They hand him the golden cup and carry the little hedgehog in triumph. Well... with thick gloves on!","Rosie stands frozen on the line, stiff as a board. Second place. She has never been second, never! It feels like a pebble in her shoe, but all over.","“That's cheating!” she fumes. “Rolling doesn't count! The grass was slippery! The sun was in my eyes!” She stomps her foot so hard that it hurts, and hops about on one paw."],["Pip potters over, clutching his cup tightly. He holds out his tiny paw. “Well done, Rosie. You run ever so fast. I just got lucky on the slope.”","Rosie glares at the outstretched paw. She crosses her arms. “I don't shake paws with cheats,” she huffs. And she turns on her heel, her nose in the air.","Behind her, Pip is left all alone with his cup. His smile goes out, like a blown-out candle."],["That evening in her bedroom, Rosie gazes at her three cups. How strange: they seem less shiny than usual. The space for the fourth is empty, and sleep will not come. So she presses her nose against the window.","Down below in the courtyard, a little light flickers. It is Pip, with his lantern. All alone in the dark, he runs, he rolls, bump! He knocks into the well, rubs his head, gets back up, and starts all over again.","“All winter long...” Rosie whispers, holding her polishing cloth in her paws. He trained all winter long. Whilst she was just polishing her cups."],["The next morning, Rosie walks across the courtyard, her paws heavy as stones. That little walk is harder than any race. She leans towards the little hedgehog and holds out her paw. “Pip...”","“You didn't cheat yesterday. You won fair and square. And I... was a very sore loser.” Looking sheepish, her ears drooping low, she offers her paw. “Well done. Truly.”","Pip shakes her paw. Ouch! It tickles and pricks just a little bit. “Will you teach me how to roll?” asks Rosie, shaking her fingers. “Only if you teach me how to turn!” giggles Pip. “I bump into everything!”"],["All through the spring, they can be seen rolling down the hill together. Rosie is dreadful at rolling: her ears stick out and flap like flags. As for Pip, he takes his corners better and better.","The following year, who won the grand race? Some swear it was Rosie. Others bet it was Pip. To tell the truth, they were both laughing so hard as they crossed the tape that nobody really noticed.","On Rosie’s shelf, there are still three cups. And in the space for the fourth, in a neat little frame, is a drawing. A bunny and a hedgehog, rolling together in the grass."]]},"rosie_espace":{"t":"Rosie and the Big Red Temper","sc":[["Today, Rosie and Bip the robot are blasting off for Marshmallow Planet. The rocket is all ready, and seatbelts are fastened tight. Rosie pushes the big green button. “One, two, three… blast off!”","Nothing. The rocket just goes “putt”, and that is all.","Rosie presses it again. “Putt.” She presses harder, with both paws. “Putt, putt.” Inside her tummy, something begins to warm up, very softly, like a saucepan on the hob."],["“Checks in progress,” announces Bip in his calm voice. “Please be patient.” Patient! Rosie hates that word. It gets hotter in her tummy. It climbs up into her chest. It bubbles away.","Her cheeks turn pink. Then red. Then tomato-red. Her ears shoot up, as stiff as two sticks. Her paws ball into tight little fists all by themselves.","It is the big red temper. When it arrives, smoke almost puffs from her ears, and Rosie cannot stop anything at all. It is just like a rocket: once launched…"],["“WORK, YOU SILLY ROCKET!” Bang! She drums on the dashboard. Bang! On the blue button, the yellow one, the purple one. Bang, bang, bang! On all the buttons at once, flashing in a panic.","“Be careful!” warns Bip, waving his arms wide. “Whatever you do, do not touch the red bu—” BANG.","A tiny click. Then a great big silence. Then Rosie feels her paws lift off the floor, and her ears float up into the air."],["She has switched off gravity: nothing stays on the floor any more. Everything floats away, the carrots, the screwdrivers, the socks, and lunchtime soup, drifting about in big orange bubbles.","Rosie spins slowly round, head down, feet in the air. She wants to bang things again, but in mid-air, there is nothing to thump. Her anger kicks at empty space.","And worst of all: Bip’s toolbox has popped open. His spare bolts scatter across the cabin like a swarm of tiny flies. Bip stretches out his little arms. Too short: he cannot catch a single one."],["“My bolts…” crackles Bip in a tiny little voice. In the muddle, a carrot has bumped his left arm. It dangles all crooked, like a broken twig.","Rosie looks at him, paws over her mouth. The red temper is still there, but behind it, something else creeps in. Something cold and very small. “Did I do that?” she whispers.","“Affirmative,” replies Bip gently. “But you were not really you. You were the anger. Would you like me to show you how I cool my circuits down?”"],["“When I get too hot,” explains Bip, “I puff. Three times. Like this.” Pfffft… On his back, a little fan begins to purr.","Rosie does not have a fan. But she has her tummy, round and soft under her paws. She closes her eyes. “One…” Pfffft. “Two…” Pfffft. “Three…” Pfffffffffft.","She feels the saucepan cool right down. Her paws uncurl. Her ears flop back down. The red temper turns pink, then teeny-tiny, and then floats out through the porthole like a little cloud, drifting away among the stars."],["“I’m sorry, Bip.” Rosie gathers the bolts one by one, like butterflies, and pops them into a jar. Then she screws the robot’s arm back on, ever so gently, poking out her tongue.","Then she presses the red button again. Clatter! Down tumbles everything at once, the socks, the screwdrivers… and the soup. Never mind: they will eat carrots instead.","“Check complete,” announces Bip, pointing to a red lever. “The rocket would not start because the handbrake was on.” Rosie bursts out laughing, clutching her tummy. All of that just for a handbrake!"],["She releases the brake. Then, with just one finger, ever so gently, she presses the green button. “One, two, three… blast off!” And WHOOSH! The rocket leaps among the stars, straight towards Marshmallow Planet.","The red temper came back, of course. It always does: when a shoelace gets tangled, when a block tower tumbles down, when you lose a game of Ludo.","But now, when Rosie feels the saucepan heating up, she closes her eyes and rests her paws on her tummy. One… two… three. And Bip, snuggled beside her, lets his little fan purr. Just to keep her company."]]},"rosie_foret":{"t":"Rosie and Her Little Gluepot","sc":[["Rosie has a little brother. His name? Pompon. He is knee-high to three carrots, has two tiny short ears, and just one big idea in life: to do everything Rosie does.","If Rosie jumps, Pompon jumps. If Rosie scratches her ear, Pompon scratches his ear. If Rosie says “Bother!”, Pompon says “Bother!”, and Rosie is the one who gets told off.","“Mummy! He won’t leave me alone!” groans Rosie. “He isn’t a brother, he’s a little gluepot!” “Gluepot!” repeats Pompon, delighted, safely snuggled against Mummy."],["Today, Rosie has a big-girl plan: with Filou and Hazel, she is going to climb to the very top of Strawberry Hill, where the biggest, sweetest wild strawberries grow.","“Me too! Me too!” begs Pompon, standing on the tips of his paws. Rosie shakes her head. “No, Pompon. It’s too far. You’re too little. You walk far too slowly.”","“Take your brother,” Mummy decides. “And hold his paw tightly.” Rosie rolls her eyes and lets out an enormous sigh. Her whole day is ruined."],["Along the path, Pompon stops at every single step. For a pebble. For an ant. For a wee. To say hello, nose-to-horns, to a snail that didn’t ask for anything at all.","“Hurry up!” Filou and Hazel are already far ahead. Rosie stamps her feet. Pompon is admiring a pebble.","Then Rosie has an idea. Not a very nice idea. “Pompon, let’s play hide-and-seek!” she suggests in a syrupy-sweet voice. “Count to ten, right here, against this big oak tree. And don’t move an inch.”"],["Pompon is overjoyed. Rosie is playing with him! Quickly, nose pressed against the bark, he covers his eyes. “One… two… three… five…”","Rosie slips away on tiptoe. Soon, she catches up with her friends. She runs, she climbs, she laughs. Free at last! “I’ll come back and find him in a bit,” she promises herself.","The strawberries are huge, red, and sweet. Rosie gobbles down one, two, ten of them, with juice dripping all over her chin. But funny thing: by the eleventh, they don’t taste like much at all."],["The sky turns orange. “Your brother’s taking an awfully long time to count,” remarks Hazel. Rosie looks down. The forest is vast, and already so dark. And Pompon is very small.","She remembers his little face earlier on. So happy to be playing. The strawberry drops from her paws. In her tummy, all the others turn into heavy stones.","Rosie rushes down the hill. She has never run so fast in her life. There is the big oak tree. She circles it. Once. Twice. Nobody. Nothing but flattened grass."],["“Pompon?” Her voice sounds tiny in the big forest. She cups her paws around her mouth. “POMPON!” Nothing. Just an owl waking up.","In the soft earth, there are pawprints, teeny-tiny ones. Rosie follows them, her nose close to the ground. They wander, they wobble, they head towards the stream. And there, caught on a bramble, is a scrap of blue cloth. His bib.","Rosie clutches it to her heart. “He isn’t a gluepot,” she says out loud. “He’s my brother.” A tear rolls down her cheek. She is very, very scared."],["A little noise. Sniff. Under a tall fern, a small white bundle is shivering. “Ten! I counted to ten,” sniffs Pompon. “Lots and lots of times. But you didn’t come. So I went looking for you.”","Rosie hugs him so tightly that he squeaks, “Squeak!” “I’m sorry, Pompon. That wasn’t real hide-and-seek. I left you all alone. It was mean. I’ll never, ever do it again.”","Pompon opens his paw. In the middle is a wild strawberry, tiny and completely squished. “I found it. It’s for you,” he announces proudly. It is the best strawberry Rosie has ever tasted."],["She carries him home on her back, through the fireflies. Long before they reach the house, he falls asleep, his nose tucked into her neck. He is quite heavy, after all, for someone only three carrots tall.","The following Sunday, Rosie goes back to Strawberry Hill. Slowly. Very slowly. Pausing for every pebble, every ant, and every snail.","“Who’s the little fellow following you everywhere?” asks a passing hedgehog. Rosie takes Pompon’s paw in hers. “Him? He’s my little gluepot. And nobody else is allowed to call him that.”"]]},"rosie_ocean":{"t":"Rosie and the Promise to Pearl","sc":[["Right at the bottom of the ocean, on Thousand Bubbles Reef, Rosie has a tiny friend: Pearl the seahorse. Pearl is so shy that she turns pink right to the tip of her tail whenever anyone looks at her.","Yet Pearl has a secret: she dances. She twirls, she glides, she makes loops of bubbles. And on Saturday, for the very first time, she will dance on the seashell stage, in front of the whole reef.","“I’ve got stage fright, Rosie, I’m so scared... Will you come? If you’re in the front row, I know I can do it.” Rosie puts a paw over her heart. “Cross my heart, I promise! I’ll be there before everyone else!”"],["Saturday arrives. Rosie is swimming towards the reef when three cheeky dolphins block her way. “Hey, speedy! Great Current Race! The winner gets this coral crown!”","A race! Rosie’s paws wriggle all on their own. “Well, the thing is... I promised a friend...” she stammers. “It only takes five minutes!” tease the dolphins. “Are you scared of losing?”","Scared of losing, her? Never in a million years! “Five minutes,” thinks Rosie. “I’ve got plenty of time. One, two, three...”"],["Zoom! The currents sweep her away in a swirl of bubbles. It’s like a water slide that never ends. She overtakes one dolphin, then two, then three. “One more lap!” they call out.","One more lap. Then a rematch. Then a rematch of the rematch. Rosie wins the coral crown! She rests it between her two ears and puffs out her chest, as proud as a queen.","Just then, she spots the light up on the surface. It isn’t yellow anymore: it’s orange. Evening is falling. Rosie slaps her paws to her cheeks. “The show!”"],["Meanwhile, back on the reef, everyone has settled into their shell. The crowd falls quiet. The seaweed curtain opens. Pearl steps onto the stage, looking tiny.","She looks at the front row. One shell has stayed empty. She looks at the second row. At the very back. Everywhere. Rosie isn’t there.","The music begins. Pearl tries to twirl. But her fins are trembling far, far too much. She turns pale, as pale as sand. And she darts off to hide behind a big yellow sponge, without having danced at all."],["Rosie swims as fast as her paws can carry her, completely out of breath. Too late: when she arrives, every shell is empty. Only Octave the octopus is still there, sweeping the stage with three of his arms.","“The show?” sighs Octave. “Oh, it didn’t happen. The little one was waiting for someone.” His round eye settles on the coral crown. “Pretty crown.”","Suddenly, the crown feels as heavy as a boulder on Rosie’s head."],["She finally tracks down Pearl, curled up right inside a pink sea anemone. “Pearl! Look, I won a crown, you can have it!” Pearl doesn’t turn around.","“I don’t want a crown. I wanted you to be there. You said cross your heart, promise.” Her little voice doesn’t shout; it barely trembles. That makes it worse.","Rosie wants to explain. The dolphins, the five minutes, the rematch. But sitting in the sand, with the crown dropped beside her, she knows deep down that all her excuses are much smaller than her promise."],["“You’re right,” whispers Rosie, her ears drooping. “A promise isn’t ‘if I have time’. I was wrong, Pearl. I can’t redo Saturday. But can I try to make it right?”","All night long, through the dark water twinkling with little lights, Rosie dashes from cave to cave. She knocks on the crabs’ door, the sunfish’s door, the old turtle’s door. “Tomorrow evening! Please, everyone come back! Please!”","The next day, Octave hangs the curtain back up with all eight arms. And Rosie arrives two hours early. She sits in the front row, paws crossed, and doesn’t budge. Not even when three dolphins swim past, whistling."],["The curtain opens. Pearl steps forward, tiny as ever. She looks at the front row... and this time, she spots two big ears, and two waving paws.","So Pearl dances. She twirls, she glides, she weaves loops of bubbles that rise and rise right to the top of the sea. Open-mouthed, the whole reef holds its breath. Rosie claps until her paws tingle.","And the coral crown? Rosie hung it above the stage, for Pearl. And from that day on, whenever she says “I promise”, she never needs to say “cross my heart”. Her word is always enough."]]},"rosie_noel":{"t":"Rosie and the Far-Too-Long Scarf","sc":[["Christmas is in three days. Rosie wants to give her mummy the loveliest present in the whole forest. Her nose pressed against Margot the magpie's shop window, she has already chosen it: a golden teapot that shines just like a little sun.","Smash! She breaks her piggy bank. Out tumble two buttons, a pebble, and a single shiny coin. But the teapot costs a hundred coins!","“You could just make her present,” suggests Pompon, pointing to the knitting basket. Rosie shrugs. Make one! A homemade present doesn't sparkle!"],["Still, she has no choice. She finds a ball of red wool, two big knitting needles, and decides to knit a scarf. “Ready, steady, go!”","Rosie knits the way she runs: at top speed. Click, click, click, click! One stitch knit, one stitch wonky, one stitch who-knows-what.","She knits in the morning, she knits at lunchtime, and again at night, tucked under her blanket with a torch. She doesn't count the rows. She has completely forgotten to stop!"],["On Christmas Eve, the scarf is finished. Rosie unrolls it. She unrolls it some more. And more. The scarf winds all the way round the bedroom. Twice!","Far too long. Much too wide at one end, skinny as a twig at the other. And right in the middle yawns a big hole, right where Pompon sneezed.","“It's ugly,” whispers Rosie. And her ears droop, all floppy and sad."],["Outside, she passes her neighbours. The hedgehogs carry a parcel tied with a silver ribbon. The squirrels have a varnished wooden music box. Everything is shiny, everything is perfect.","Rosie hides her scarf behind her back. Her cheeks burn hot. “Mummy deserved a golden teapot,” she mumbles. “And she's getting… this.”","That evening, she stuffs the scarf right at the bottom of the cupboard, under a heap of old socks. “I'll just say I ran out of time,” she decides."],["On Christmas morning, everyone hugs. Mummy unfolds Pompon's drawing: a big green scribble. “Oh! A magnificent… Christmas tree?” “It's you, Mummy!” “Ah. Yes, it looks just like me.”","“And what about you, my Rosie?” Rosie stares down at her paws. “I… haven't got anything,” she stammers. “I ran out of time.”","“Yes she has!” pipes up Pompon. “She made a great big red snake! It's in the cupboard!” And before Rosie can stop him, back he comes, dragging the scarf behind him in a shower of socks."],["Rosie wishes she could vanish under the rug. “Don't look,” she pleads. “It's ruined. Too long, and it's got a hole. I wanted to give you the golden teapot, but…”","Mummy stays quiet. Slowly, she runs her paws over the wool. Over every wonky stitch. “Did you make this all by yourself? All those nights when I saw light under your blanket?”","Rosie nods, feeling very sheepish. And then she notices something wonderful: Mummy's eyes are shining. Much brighter than the teapot."],["Mummy winds the scarf round her neck. Once. Twice. There is still plenty left, trailing along the floor like a giant ribbon. So she winds it round Rosie. And then round Pompon.","“Look. A teapot is only for one person. Your scarf keeps all three of us warm. And the hole…” Pompon pokes his head through, giggling. “It's my window!”","Off they go for a walk in the snow, all three bundled up in the same red scarf. From afar, they look just like a funny six-legged creature."],["They stroll past Margot's shop window. The golden teapot is still there. Rosie turns her head. Yes, it sparkles. But it looks rather cold, all by itself behind the glass.","All winter long, Mummy wears the scarf. To go to the market, to hang out the washing, and even once to bed. If anyone tells her it is too long, she proudly replies: “It's just the right size.”","And what about Rosie? She has picked up her needles again. Click… click… click. Slower this time, poking her tongue out. She is knitting mittens for Pompon. So far, there is one. With six fingers."]]},"rosie_halloween":{"t":"Rosie and the Melted Costume","sc":[["This year, Rosie is going to win the Halloween fancy-dress competition. It is decided! For a whole month, she has been working in secret, by lamplight, in the garden shed.","It is a dragon. A magnificent dragon, made entirely of paper: green scales glued on one by one, a spiky tail, and wings that fan out wide.","“It is perfect,” Rosie cheers, her paws on her hips. “Not a single scale out of place. Nobody has ever seen a costume like this!”"],["On Halloween night, she slips into her dragon suit. “Ready, steady, go!” Off she goes to the clearing. As she passes, everybody turns to look. “Oooh!” Rosie struts along, her head held high.","She does not see the big black cloud gathering behind her. Plip! A drop on her nose. Plip, plop! Two drops on her wings.","And suddenly, all the rain in the sky comes pouring down on the forest."],["Rosie dashes away. But paper does not like water! The scales peel off, one by one. The green paint trickles down her legs. The tail dangles like an old soggy sock.","When she finally shelters under the great oak tree, she is no longer a dragon. She is a completely green bunny, soaked to the bone, with bits of soggy paper stuck all over her.","“My dragon! A whole month’s work! It’s completely ruined! This is the worst night of my life!” She flops down into a puddle, her ears flat. Rosie doesn't want anything anymore."],["Her friends come running up. “Take my pumpkin hat,” offers Nestor. “And my pirate bandana!” adds Filou. Plume, without a word, holds out her witch’s cloak.","“No! That’s not what I wanted! I wanted MY dragon, just like I planned!” Rosie crosses her arms and turns her back on them. “I’m going home. Halloween is cancelled.”","From the corner of her eye, she looks at her green paws. The soggy bits of paper. The cloak, the bandana, the hat, still held out by her friends. Inside her head, a tiny little idea begins to sprout."],["She peers into the puddle. Looking back at her is a dripping, green creature covered in tatters. It isn’t a dragon. It is… something else.","“Well now,” whispers Rosie. “I look just like a swamp monster.” In one swift move, she throws the cloak over her shoulders. Then the bandana. And the pumpkin hat on top! “A swamp monster who is a witch and a pirate.”","Filou giggles. “With some seaweed, it would be even better!” Rosie scoops up a handful of wet leaves and slaps them onto her ears. Splat!"],["Everyone joins in! Nestor draws muddy whiskers on her cheeks. Plume gives her an acorn necklace. Rosie sticks a twig onto her head for a horn.","The more they add, the more dreadful it looks. The more dreadful it looks, the more they roar with laughter! Rosie hadn't planned any of this. And that is exactly what makes it so funny.","“What is your monster called?” asks Plume. Rosie thinks for a moment, then lifts her paw solemnly. “The Swamp Gloobidragon. It only exists one night a year. And only when it rains!”"],["The rain stops. The competition begins. Across the stage parade very tidy ghosts, neatly pressed witches, and an immaculate skeleton.","Then the Gloobidragon clambers onto the stage, leaving little green puddles behind it. It lets out a roar: “GLOOOBYYY!” The little ones shriek with fright and delight.","Old Mathurine, who is head of the judges, gives her spectacles a long wipe. “In a hundred years of competitions, I have never seen anything quite so disgusting. Bravo!”"],["Did Rosie win? She was awarded the prize for Most Original Monster! First prize went to the skeleton. But splashing in her green bath, watched by Pompon, Rosie doesn't mind a bit.","That evening, it takes three whole baths to wash away all the green. The bathwater looks just like spinach soup. Pompon pokes his tongue out, wanting a taste!","From her dragon, Rosie kept just one crinkly scale at the bottom of a box. Next year, she will make another perfect costume. But she will pack a little pot of mud. Just in case."]]},"rosie_ski":{"t":"Rosie, Bottom of the Class","sc":[["Rosie runs faster than anyone. She jumps higher, she swims further. So when she arrives at White Pines Resort, she doesn't have the slightest doubt. “Skiing? I’ll be the best by lunchtime! Ready, steady, go!”","She clicks into her skis, stands up tall. And whoosh—her two paws shoot off in different directions, as if they’re having a row. Thud!","She gets back up. Thud! She gets up again. Thud! “These skis are broken!” she grumbles. “No,” smiles Mr Cabri. “They’re brand-new. You’re the one who is new.”"],["The worst part is everyone else. Nestor, who is usually so slow, glides peacefully along like a big boat. Plume carves neat little turns. Even Pompon stays on his feet!","And Rosie, the fastest in the forest, spends her morning with her bottom in the snow, watching tiny toddlers whizz past. She is last. The very last in the class.","“Take your time,” Nestor tells her kindly. “Do what I do.” Taking advice from Nestor! Rosie grits her teeth. Smoke practically puffs from her ears."],["At lunchtime, she throws down her ski poles. “Skiing is rubbish. It’s a silly sport. I am never doing it again!” And off she goes to sulk on the bench outside the chalet.","From there, she watches the others. They are laughing. They fall over too, actually. Nestor tumbles. Plume tumbles. But they just giggle and get back up.","“How can they laugh?” Rosie mutters, curling into a ball. Whenever she isn't the best, she feels like she isn't anything at all."],["Mr Cabri comes and sits beside her with two steaming mugs of hot chocolate. “Do you know why Nestor manages so well?” “Because he’s good at it.” “No. Because he’s used to not getting things right the first time.”","“You are always first. You have never learned how to be a beginner. It’s a real job, you know. And the very best one.”","“The best one? Tumbling over all the time?” Rosie asks, surprised. “Of course. When you’re a champion, you hardly improve at all. When you’re a beginner, you make a little miracle happen every single day.”"],["Rosie finishes her hot chocolate. She looks at her skis, stuck in the snow. A big sigh. And back she goes. “Just so you know, I am going to fall over.” “I should hope so!” replies Mr Cabri.","She falls. Once, twice, ten times. But now, she’s counting. “Eleven!” shouts Pompon. “Twelve!” On number thirteen, she glides for three whole metres before tumbling over.","Three metres! “Did you see? Did you see?” she beams. She has snow right up to her ears, and a grin from ear to ear."],["The next day, she stays upright all the way to the pine tree. The day after that, she makes her first turn. She didn't do it on purpose, but it counts all the same!","When she falls, Nestor reaches out a paw. She takes it. “How do you bend your knees like that?” It is the first time in her life that Rosie has ever asked for advice.","“As if you’re sitting on a tiny little stool,” Nestor explains. She tries it. It works! “Nestor, you’re a genius!” And Nestor blushes all the way up to his bobble hat."],["At the end of the week comes the Snowflake test. You have to ski all the way down the green slope without falling once. In her chest, Rosie’s heart goes thump, thump, thump.","Off she goes. A snowplough. A turn. Another turn. She skis down slowly, much slower than the others, with her tongue poked out in concentration. She finishes last. But she finishes on her feet!","Mr Cabri pins a little silver snowflake onto her scarf. Rosie has won cups, medals, and crowns before. But none of them had ever felt like this."],["On the final morning, a tiny little marmot arrives at the ski school. She clicks into her skis. Thud! She gets back up. Thud! “These skis are broken!” she whimpers.","Rosie glides over in a snowplough. She crouches down. “No. They’re brand-new. You’re the one who is new. And you’re lucky—it’s the best job in the whole world.”","“Come on, I’ll count with you. One!” Thud! “Two!” Thud! And across the beginners’ slope echo two big bursts of laughter, with both bottoms plopped right in the snow."]]},"rosie_mer":{"t":"Rosie and the Dancing Crab","sc":[["At low tide, Dunes Beach is full of treasures. Puddles, rocks, and seaweed that goes pop when you step on it. Rosie explores every little corner, bucket in paw.","And on top of a rock, she spots the funniest thing in the whole wide world. A little red crab, dancing! Three steps to the left, three steps to the right, and click, clack! go his claws.","“Ooh! I want him! Ready, steady, go!” Rosie is as quick as a flash. Whoosh! The crab is in the bucket before his dance is even finished."],["“Look! I’ve got a crab!” cheers Rosie. “His name is Castanet, and he’s mine. I’m taking him home, and he will dance for me every single day.”","She lines the bucket with sand. Then she adds a seashell, just to make it pretty. And a piece of biscuit. “You’ll be ever so cosy here. Go on, dance!” And she claps her paws.","Castanet doesn't dance. He walks round the bucket. Round and round again. Then he stretches his claws towards the rim, way up high."],["That evening, in the beach hut, Rosie puts the bucket on the table. “Dance, Castanet! One, two, three!” Nothing. She taps the bucket. Tap, tap. The crab scuttles under the seashell.","“Is your crab broken?” asks Pompon. “Of course not,” replies Rosie. “He’s just tired. He’ll dance tomorrow.”","But the next day, Castanet still isn't dancing. He hasn't touched the biscuit. And he isn't even very red anymore: he has turned a pale, sad little pink."],["Plume leans over the bucket. She watches for a long time, as she does so well. “Rosie… have you seen where he’s looking?”","The crab’s two little eyes, on the tips of their stalks, are turned towards the window. Towards the sound of the waves. They don't move at all.","“He was dancing on his rock,” whispers Plume. “I don't think he was dancing for you. I think he was dancing because he was home.”"],["“But I’m taking good care of him!” protests Rosie. “He has sand, a shell, and a biscuit!” She hugs the bucket tightly, looking stubborn. “And anyway, I found him first.”","That night, a tiny sound wakes her. Scritch. Scritch. It’s Castanet, trying to climb the side of the bucket. He slips. He tries again. He slips once more.","Rosie wonders what she would do if a giant popped her into a bucket. Even a very pretty bucket. Even with crunchy carrots. She wouldn't feel like running around either."],["Early the next morning, before the cottage wakes up, she takes the bucket and heads down to the beach. The tide is out. The rock is right there, all shiny and wet.","“I wanted to keep you because you were lovely,” she whispers. “But at my house, you aren't lovely at all. You’re just sad. I’m sorry, Castanet.”","She tilts the bucket, ever so gently. The little crab hesitates on the rim. Then he slides onto the rock, feeling the salty water under his feet once more. Rosie holds her breath."],["At first, he doesn't move. Then one leg moves. Then another. Three steps to the left. Three steps to the right. And click, clack! His claws snap in the morning sunshine.","Out of a crack pops a second crab. Then a third. A whole merry troupe, forming a circle around him and waving their claws! Castanet has turned bright cherry-red again.","Rosie sits down on the wet sand, hugging her knees. She doesn't touch a thing. She just watches. It is ever so much lovelier than inside the bucket."],["Every morning of the holidays, Rosie goes back to the rock at low tide. She sits as quiet as a mouse. And nearly every time, Castanet comes to dance. Not for her. But not far away.","She has turned her bucket upside down. It makes a wonderful stool for peering into rock pools. Inside it, she only ever brings home empty shells, where nobody lives.","And when a toddler arrives with a fishing net, shouting, “Ooh! A crab! I want him!”, Rosie makes room for them on the sand. “Shh. Sit down. Look. He’s going to dance.”"]]}};
